1
00:00:00,041 --> 00:00:00,041
23.976

2
00:00:58,892 --> 00:01:03,563
NORTH BY NORTHWEST

3
00:02:27,355 --> 00:02:31,234
"Even if you accept the belief that a high
Trendex means a rising sales curve...."

4
00:02:31,276 --> 00:02:32,777
-Mr. Thornhill?
-Good night, Eddie.

5
00:02:32,819 --> 00:02:35,280
-Say hello to the missus.
-We're not talking.

6
00:02:35,321 --> 00:02:37,282
"My recommendation is still the same.

7
00:02:37,365 --> 00:02:40,410
"Spread the good word in as many
small time segments as we can.

8
00:02:40,493 --> 00:02:44,289
"Let the opposition have their high ratings
while we cry all the way to the bank.

9
00:02:44,372 --> 00:02:47,167
"Let's colonize at the Colony
one day next week for lunch.

10
00:02:47,250 --> 00:02:49,836
"Let me hear from you, Sam.
Happy thoughts, etc...."

11
00:02:49,919 --> 00:02:51,504
You better walk me to the Plaza.

12
00:02:51,588 --> 00:02:54,799
-I didn't put a coat on.
-Use your blood sugar, child. Come on.

13
00:02:54,883 --> 00:02:56,384
-Next?
-Gretchen Sabinson.

14
00:02:56,468 --> 00:02:57,177
Oh, yes.

15
00:02:57,260 --> 00:03:00,180
Send her a box of candy from Blum's.
$10. You know the kind.

16
00:03:00,263 --> 00:03:03,808
Each piece wrapped in gold paper.
She'll think she's eating money.

17
00:03:03,892 --> 00:03:06,644
Just tell her:
"Darling, I count the days, the hours--"

18
00:03:06,686 --> 00:03:08,772
-You sent that one last time.
-I did?

19
00:03:08,813 --> 00:03:12,942
Put: "Something for your sweet tooth,
baby, and all your other sweet parts! "

20
00:03:13,151 --> 00:03:13,985
I know.

21
00:03:14,069 --> 00:03:15,320
Could we take a cab?

22
00:03:15,403 --> 00:03:17,572
-For two blocks?
-You're late and I'm tired.

23
00:03:17,655 --> 00:03:20,325
That's your trouble, Maggie,
you don't eat properly.

24
00:03:20,408 --> 00:03:21,451
Here. Taxi.

25
00:03:22,535 --> 00:03:25,246
I have a very sick woman here.
You don't mind, do you?

26
00:03:25,330 --> 00:03:26,539
Thank you very much.

27
00:03:26,623 --> 00:03:28,124
Perfectly all right.

28
00:03:28,541 --> 00:03:30,835
First stop, the Plaza. Don't throw the flag.

29
00:03:30,877 --> 00:03:31,878
Poor man.

30
00:03:33,046 --> 00:03:36,257
I made him a happy man.
I made him feel like a good Samaritan.

31
00:03:36,341 --> 00:03:38,009
He knew you were lying.

32
00:03:39,260 --> 00:03:42,138
In the world of advertising,
there's no such thing as a lie.

33
00:03:42,180 --> 00:03:45,642
There's only "the expedient exaggeration."
You ought to know that.

34
00:03:46,059 --> 00:03:48,019
Do I look heavyish to you?

35
00:03:48,103 --> 00:03:49,020
What?

36
00:03:49,062 --> 00:03:50,438
I feel heavyish.

37
00:03:50,563 --> 00:03:53,233
Put a note on my desk in the morning:
"Think thin."

38
00:03:53,316 --> 00:03:54,609
"Think thin."

39
00:03:55,235 --> 00:03:57,529
Better make it the 59th Street entrance.

40
00:03:57,570 --> 00:03:58,363
Okay.

41
00:03:59,406 --> 00:04:01,366
Soon as you get back, call my mother.

42
00:04:01,408 --> 00:04:03,827
Remind her we have theatre tickets
for tonight.

43
00:04:03,868 --> 00:04:05,704
Dinner at 21, 7:00.

44
00:04:05,829 --> 00:04:09,958
I'll have had two martini's at the Oak Bar
so she needn't bother to sniff my breath.

45
00:04:10,041 --> 00:04:12,836
-She doesn't do that.
-Sure she does. Like a bloodhound.

46
00:04:12,877 --> 00:04:16,673
Bigelow at 10:30 is your first for tomorrow.
The Skin Glow rehearsal's at noon.

47
00:04:16,756 --> 00:04:18,675
Then lunch with Falcon and his wife.

48
00:04:18,758 --> 00:04:21,344
-Where was that?
-Larry and Arnold's, 1 :00.

49
00:04:21,761 --> 00:04:24,264
-Will you check in later?
-Absolutely not.

50
00:04:25,682 --> 00:04:28,393
Here, Driver. Take this lady
back where she belongs.

51
00:04:28,476 --> 00:04:30,478
-Right.
-That ought to cover it.

52
00:04:31,312 --> 00:04:33,773
-Don't forget, call my mother right away.
-I won't.

53
00:04:33,857 --> 00:04:36,568
-Good night, Mr. Thornhill.
-Good night.

54
00:04:39,446 --> 00:04:42,449
Wait, Maggie! You can't call her.
She's at Mrs.--

55
00:05:04,763 --> 00:05:06,181
Good evening, Mr. Thornhill.

56
00:05:06,264 --> 00:05:08,683
I'm looking for Mr. Weltner
and two other gentlemen.

57
00:05:08,767 --> 00:05:10,477
Yes, sir. Right this way.

58
00:05:11,561 --> 00:05:12,312
Herman.

59
00:05:12,395 --> 00:05:14,105
-Hello, Roger.
-I'm a little late.

60
00:05:14,189 --> 00:05:15,940
Roger Thornhill. Fanning Nelson.

61
00:05:18,026 --> 00:05:21,279
-We've gotten a head start here.
-That won't last long.

62
00:05:21,363 --> 00:05:25,784
I was saying that you may be slow
in starting, but there's nobody faster.

63
00:05:25,867 --> 00:05:28,036
What's the matter? You've got the fidgets.

64
00:05:28,119 --> 00:05:32,957
I told my secretary to call Mother and
I realized she won't be able to reach her.

65
00:05:33,208 --> 00:05:33,958
Why not?

66
00:05:34,042 --> 00:05:36,294
She's playing bridge at one of her cronies'.

67
00:05:36,336 --> 00:05:38,046
-Your secretary?
-No. My mother.

68
00:05:38,129 --> 00:05:39,047
Mr. Kaplan?

69
00:05:39,130 --> 00:05:42,258
It's one of those new apartments,
wet paint and no telephone yet.

70
00:05:42,467 --> 00:05:44,469
Perhaps if I send her a telegram.

71
00:05:44,511 --> 00:05:45,887
Mr. George Kaplan?

72
00:05:45,970 --> 00:05:46,805
Boy!

73
00:05:52,602 --> 00:05:53,353
Kaplan.

74
00:05:53,436 --> 00:05:54,938
I have to send a wire.

75
00:05:55,021 --> 00:05:57,482
Could you send it for me
if I write it out for you?

76
00:05:57,565 --> 00:06:00,652
I'm not permitted to do that, sir,
but if you'll follow me....

77
00:06:00,819 --> 00:06:02,987
-Will you excuse me?
-Go right ahead.

78
00:06:06,616 --> 00:06:08,451
-Right through there, sir.
-Thanks.

79
00:06:08,493 --> 00:06:09,828
Thank you, sir.

80
00:06:12,163 --> 00:06:13,873
What's that supposed to be?

81
00:06:13,957 --> 00:06:17,460
The car is waiting outside.
You'll walk between us saying nothing.

82
00:06:17,502 --> 00:06:19,838
-What are you talking about?
-Let's go.

83
00:06:20,130 --> 00:06:21,506
Go where? Who....

84
00:06:22,173 --> 00:06:23,091
Who are you?

85
00:06:23,174 --> 00:06:25,385
Mere errand boys,
carrying concealed weapons.

86
00:06:25,468 --> 00:06:29,389
His is pointed at your heart, so, please,
no errors of judgment, I beg of you.

87
00:06:29,472 --> 00:06:31,307
What is this? A joke or something?

88
00:06:31,349 --> 00:06:33,810
Yes, a joke. We'll laugh in the car.

89
00:06:34,019 --> 00:06:34,853
Come.

90
00:06:34,936 --> 00:06:36,396
This is ridiculous.

91
00:06:57,459 --> 00:07:00,503
Don't tell me where we're going.
Surprise me.

92
00:07:02,422 --> 00:07:05,175
I left some friends
back there in the Oak Bar.

93
00:07:05,216 --> 00:07:07,218
They'll think I'm awfully rude.

94
00:07:07,302 --> 00:07:10,221
Could we stop off
at a drugstore for a moment...

95
00:07:10,305 --> 00:07:13,516
...so that I could explain
I'm being kidnapped?

96
00:07:14,726 --> 00:07:17,228
Well, that is what's happening, isn't it?

97
00:07:26,571 --> 00:07:27,614
Locked.

98
00:07:31,951 --> 00:07:33,787
TOWNSEND

99
00:07:40,710 --> 00:07:42,045
Who's Townsend?

100
00:07:44,464 --> 00:07:45,465
Really?

101
00:07:46,257 --> 00:07:47,425
Interesting.

102
00:08:31,970 --> 00:08:33,972
-Where is he?
-Upstairs, dressing.

103
00:08:34,055 --> 00:08:36,599
-Tell him I'm here.
-The dinner guests are expected.

104
00:08:36,641 --> 00:08:38,727
Never mind that. Say to him: "Kaplan."

105
00:08:38,810 --> 00:08:41,146
By the way,
what are we having for dessert?

106
00:08:41,187 --> 00:08:42,897
-Is anyone in the library?
-No.

107
00:08:42,981 --> 00:08:43,982
This way.

108
00:08:55,660 --> 00:08:56,828
You will wait here.

109
00:08:56,911 --> 00:08:59,873
Don't hurry. I'll catch up on my reading.

110
00:09:19,309 --> 00:09:21,686
MR. LESTER TOWNSEND
169 BAYWOOD, GLEN COVE, N.Y.

111
00:09:42,707 --> 00:09:44,084
Good evening.

112
00:10:07,190 --> 00:10:09,609
Not what I expected. A little taller...

113
00:10:10,694 --> 00:10:13,196
...a little more polished than the others.

114
00:10:14,155 --> 00:10:17,033
I'm so glad you're pleased, Mr. Townsend.

115
00:10:17,075 --> 00:10:19,494
But, I'm afraid, just as obvious.

116
00:10:20,995 --> 00:10:23,873
What the devil is all this about?
Why was I brought here?

117
00:10:26,042 --> 00:10:28,044
Games? Must we?

118
00:10:29,838 --> 00:10:34,342
Not that I mind a slight case of abduction,
but I have tickets for the theatre tonight.

119
00:10:34,426 --> 00:10:36,261
To a show I was looking forward to.

120
00:10:36,302 --> 00:10:38,805
I get unreasonable about things like that.

121
00:10:39,264 --> 00:10:43,018
With such expert play-acting,
you make this very room a theatre.

122
00:10:44,352 --> 00:10:47,564
Leonard, have you met
our distinguished guest?

123
00:10:49,482 --> 00:10:51,317
He's a well-tailored one, isn't he?

124
00:10:51,735 --> 00:10:55,030
My secretary is a great admirer
of your methods, Mr. Kaplan.

125
00:10:55,447 --> 00:10:57,282
Elusiveness, however misguided--

126
00:10:57,365 --> 00:10:58,783
Wait a minute.

127
00:10:59,534 --> 00:11:00,952
Did you call me "Kaplan"?

128
00:11:01,036 --> 00:11:05,415
I know you're a man of many names,
but I'll accept your current choice.

129
00:11:05,540 --> 00:11:06,791
Current choice?

130
00:11:06,958 --> 00:11:10,503
My name is Roger Thornhill.
It's never been anything else.

131
00:11:10,587 --> 00:11:11,796
Of course.

132
00:11:12,589 --> 00:11:16,509
Your friends picked up the wrong package
when they bundled me out here in the car.

133
00:11:16,593 --> 00:11:18,386
Sit down, Mr. Kaplan.

134
00:11:18,803 --> 00:11:21,681
I told you, I'm not Kaplan, whoever he is.

135
00:11:22,390 --> 00:11:23,641
-Excuse me.
-Yes?

136
00:11:23,683 --> 00:11:25,101
The guests are here, dear.

137
00:11:25,143 --> 00:11:27,937
Look after them.
I'll be with you in a few minutes.

138
00:11:30,231 --> 00:11:32,359
Now, shall we get down to business?

139
00:11:32,442 --> 00:11:33,526
I'm all for that.

140
00:11:33,610 --> 00:11:37,072
Simply, I'd like you to tell me
how much you know of our arrangements...

141
00:11:37,155 --> 00:11:39,282
...and how you've come by
this information.

142
00:11:39,366 --> 00:11:41,993
-I don't expect to get this for nothing.
-Of course not.

143
00:11:42,077 --> 00:11:43,203
Don't misunderstand.

144
00:11:43,286 --> 00:11:45,872
I don't expect you to fall in
with the suggestion...

145
00:11:45,955 --> 00:11:50,335
...but the least I can do is afford you
the opportunity of surviving the evening.

146
00:11:53,672 --> 00:11:55,632
What is that supposed to mean?

147
00:11:55,674 --> 00:11:58,426
Why don't you surprise me,
Mr. Kaplan, and say yes?

148
00:11:58,510 --> 00:11:59,636
I've told you--

149
00:11:59,678 --> 00:12:01,388
We know where you're headed for.

150
00:12:01,471 --> 00:12:04,849
I know where I'm headed.
The Winter Garden Theatre in New York.

151
00:12:04,891 --> 00:12:07,102
And I think I'd better get going.

152
00:12:15,652 --> 00:12:17,779
Townsend,
you're making a serious mistake.

153
00:12:18,279 --> 00:12:21,825
This is not going to lead
to a very happy conclusion, Mr. Kaplan.

154
00:12:22,075 --> 00:12:23,535
I'm not Kaplan!

155
00:12:23,576 --> 00:12:25,245
I do wish you would reconsider.

156
00:12:25,328 --> 00:12:29,332
We also know your contact in Pittsburgh
since Jason committed suicide.

157
00:12:30,041 --> 00:12:33,086
What contact?
I've never been in Pittsburgh.

158
00:12:34,004 --> 00:12:37,465
On June 16, you checked
into the Sherwyn Hotel in Pittsburgh...

159
00:12:37,549 --> 00:12:40,010
...as Mr. George Kaplan
of Berkeley, California.

160
00:12:40,093 --> 00:12:43,054
A week later, you registered
at the Benjamin Franklin Hotel...

161
00:12:43,096 --> 00:12:45,598
...in Philadelphia
as Mr. George Kaplan of Pittsburgh.

162
00:12:45,682 --> 00:12:47,892
On August 11,
you stayed at the Statler in Boston.

163
00:12:47,976 --> 00:12:52,022
On August 29, George Kaplan of Boston
registered at the Whittier in Detroit.

164
00:12:52,063 --> 00:12:54,399
At present, you are registered
in Room 796...

165
00:12:54,482 --> 00:12:58,069
...at the Plaza Hotel in New York
as Mr. George Kaplan of Detroit.

166
00:12:58,236 --> 00:13:01,948
In two days, you are due
at the Ambassador East in Chicago.

167
00:13:02,365 --> 00:13:05,952
And then at the Sheraton-Johnson Hotel
in Rapid City, South Dakota.

168
00:13:06,036 --> 00:13:06,786
Not me!

169
00:13:06,870 --> 00:13:09,956
There's no sense in maintaining
that you are deceiving us...

170
00:13:10,040 --> 00:13:12,125
...any more than we are deceiving you.

171
00:13:12,208 --> 00:13:15,337
I suppose it wouldn't do any good
to show you ID cards...

172
00:13:15,420 --> 00:13:17,297
...a driver's license, things like that?

173
00:13:17,380 --> 00:13:19,299
They provide you with such good ones.

174
00:13:20,383 --> 00:13:22,510
It's getting late. I have guests.

175
00:13:23,094 --> 00:13:26,598
Do you intend to cooperate with us?
I'd like a simple yes or no.

176
00:13:26,723 --> 00:13:27,932
A simple no.

177
00:13:28,099 --> 00:13:32,103
For the simple reason that I simply
don't know what you're talking about.

178
00:13:36,024 --> 00:13:38,109
Give Mr. Kaplan a drink, Leonard.

179
00:13:38,318 --> 00:13:40,028
A pleasant journey, sir.

180
00:13:51,164 --> 00:13:52,916
Scotch? Rye?

181
00:13:53,083 --> 00:13:54,793
Bourbon? Vodka?

182
00:13:55,377 --> 00:13:57,712
Nothing. I'll take a quick ride
back to town.

183
00:13:57,796 --> 00:13:59,464
That has been arranged.

184
00:13:59,547 --> 00:14:01,966
But first, a libation.

185
00:14:03,259 --> 00:14:04,260
Bourbon!

186
00:14:04,928 --> 00:14:07,847
You drink it.
I've had enough stimulation for one day.

187
00:14:07,931 --> 00:14:12,352
It'll be easier if you take this yourself.
Otherwise, we'll have to insist.

188
00:14:26,658 --> 00:14:27,701
Cheers!

189
00:15:16,458 --> 00:15:18,585
Don't worry about me. I'll take the bus.

190
00:18:10,465 --> 00:18:11,966
Thanks for the lift.

191
00:18:12,050 --> 00:18:14,886
I want this man examined for driving
while intoxicated.

192
00:18:14,928 --> 00:18:16,012
Really?

193
00:18:16,262 --> 00:18:18,348
You see, they tried to kill me.

194
00:18:18,765 --> 00:18:20,225
He won't listen to me.

195
00:18:20,266 --> 00:18:22,602
In a big house. They tried to kill me.

196
00:18:22,686 --> 00:18:25,814
-All right. Let's just go inside.
-I don't want to go inside.

197
00:18:25,897 --> 00:18:27,315
Somebody call the police.

198
00:18:27,399 --> 00:18:28,983
Come on. Come on now.

199
00:18:29,776 --> 00:18:30,860
Okay.

200
00:18:39,577 --> 00:18:40,745
Sit down.

201
00:18:41,746 --> 00:18:43,248
I don't want to sit.

202
00:18:43,748 --> 00:18:45,500
Perfectly all right. See?

203
00:18:45,875 --> 00:18:49,879
We'll get 'em. We'll throw the book
at them. Assault and kidnapping.

204
00:18:50,130 --> 00:18:52,632
Assault with a gun and a bourbon
and a sports car.

205
00:18:52,716 --> 00:18:53,717
We'll get 'em.

206
00:18:53,758 --> 00:18:57,470
You'll be fine after a good night's sleep.
We got a nice cell all made up.

207
00:18:57,595 --> 00:18:59,723
I don't want a cell. I want a policeman.

208
00:18:59,806 --> 00:19:01,933
The car was just reported stolen.

209
00:19:03,184 --> 00:19:05,311
A Mrs. Babson up on Twining Road.

210
00:19:06,062 --> 00:19:08,231
I got to call someone. Where's the phone?

211
00:19:08,273 --> 00:19:09,941
You're allowed one call.

212
00:19:10,442 --> 00:19:11,568
Over here.

213
00:19:11,693 --> 00:19:12,736
Thanks.

214
00:19:13,236 --> 00:19:15,613
You better make it your lawyer.

215
00:19:16,156 --> 00:19:18,324
Butterfield 8-1-0-9-8.

216
00:19:18,533 --> 00:19:20,577
What am l, a telephone operator?

217
00:19:20,660 --> 00:19:22,412
Butterfield 8-1-0-9-8.

218
00:19:35,884 --> 00:19:37,344
Just a minute, please.

219
00:19:37,427 --> 00:19:39,012
-Here.
-Thank you.

220
00:19:41,473 --> 00:19:42,724
Hello, Mother?

221
00:19:43,725 --> 00:19:46,478
Mother, this is your son, Roger Thornhill.

222
00:19:49,022 --> 00:19:50,940
Wait a minute, I'll find out.

223
00:19:51,691 --> 00:19:52,942
Where am l?

224
00:19:52,984 --> 00:19:55,028
Glen Cove Police Station.

225
00:19:55,653 --> 00:19:57,822
Glen Cove Police Station.

226
00:19:59,741 --> 00:20:02,202
No, Mother, I have not been drinking.

227
00:20:02,452 --> 00:20:06,956
No, these two men, they poured
a whole bottle of bourbon into me.

228
00:20:08,458 --> 00:20:10,543
No, they didn't give me a chaser.

229
00:20:12,170 --> 00:20:13,838
Come on, let's go.

230
00:20:15,340 --> 00:20:18,343
-Wait, I'm not finished yet.
-Yes, you are. Come on.

231
00:20:18,802 --> 00:20:21,554
Mother, I got to go now.

232
00:20:22,013 --> 00:20:24,683
Get my lawyer right away
and come and bail me out.

233
00:20:24,766 --> 00:20:26,685
Tomorrow morning, tell her.

234
00:20:26,976 --> 00:20:28,687
Tomorrow morning, he says.

235
00:20:30,021 --> 00:20:31,314
I don't know. I'll ask him.

236
00:20:31,356 --> 00:20:33,149
She wants to know who says.

237
00:20:33,191 --> 00:20:35,068
Sergeant Emil Klinger.

238
00:20:35,402 --> 00:20:36,903
Sergeant Emil....

239
00:20:37,487 --> 00:20:38,488
Emil?

240
00:20:40,073 --> 00:20:41,950
Sergeant Emil Klinger.

241
00:20:43,660 --> 00:20:45,662
No. I didn't believe it either.

242
00:20:47,205 --> 00:20:50,041
I'm all right, Mother. Good night.

243
00:20:55,046 --> 00:20:56,423
That was Mother.

244
00:20:57,173 --> 00:20:58,258
Let's go.

245
00:21:05,015 --> 00:21:06,683
Here's your man, Doctor.

246
00:21:09,978 --> 00:21:11,021
What's your name?

247
00:21:11,271 --> 00:21:12,814
Roger Thornhill.

248
00:21:13,064 --> 00:21:15,150
Stick out your tongue and say: "ah."

249
00:21:15,233 --> 00:21:16,776
You better move back.

250
00:21:18,737 --> 00:21:20,280
Have you been drinking?

251
00:21:20,363 --> 00:21:22,407
Doctor, I am gassed.

252
00:21:23,033 --> 00:21:24,367
What were you drinking?

253
00:21:24,409 --> 00:21:26,619
Well, bourbon. See, these two fellas--

254
00:21:26,703 --> 00:21:29,372
-How much would you say you drank?
-What?

255
00:21:29,414 --> 00:21:32,667
-How much would you say you drank?
-About this much.

256
00:21:33,293 --> 00:21:37,005
Mr. Thornhill, my opinion
is that you are definitely intoxicated.

257
00:21:37,088 --> 00:21:38,298
No question about it.

258
00:21:38,381 --> 00:21:40,550
I won't ask your permission to draw blood.

259
00:21:40,592 --> 00:21:41,634
How disgusting!

260
00:21:41,718 --> 00:21:44,220
"You may refuse to permit a blood test
to be made...

261
00:21:44,304 --> 00:21:46,806
"...but if you do,
your license will be revoked."

262
00:21:49,142 --> 00:21:52,437
It was at this point
that Mr. Thornhill succeeded in escaping...

263
00:21:52,479 --> 00:21:55,565
...from his would-be assassins,
and when they gave chase...

264
00:21:55,648 --> 00:21:59,736
...he, naturally, had to drive
as best he could under the circumstances.

265
00:22:00,987 --> 00:22:02,614
How long have you known your client?

266
00:22:02,822 --> 00:22:04,240
Seven years, Your Honor.

267
00:22:04,282 --> 00:22:06,451
Do you know him to be a reasonable man?

268
00:22:06,701 --> 00:22:07,994
Absolutely.

269
00:22:09,954 --> 00:22:10,955
Mother!

270
00:22:11,206 --> 00:22:14,125
Do you believe there is some credence
to this story?

271
00:22:14,626 --> 00:22:15,502
Credence!

272
00:22:15,585 --> 00:22:18,672
Yes, Your Honor. If my client says
this is what happened...

273
00:22:18,797 --> 00:22:20,632
...I'm certain it must've happened.

274
00:22:20,674 --> 00:22:21,591
Damn right.

275
00:22:22,676 --> 00:22:26,054
I want this turned over
to the County Detectives for investigation.

276
00:22:26,137 --> 00:22:28,390
Call them and have them
come here immediately.

277
00:22:28,473 --> 00:22:29,557
Right, Your Honor.

278
00:22:29,641 --> 00:22:34,396
Counselor, I'm going to set this over
for final disposition tomorrow at 7:30 p.m.

279
00:22:35,146 --> 00:22:38,775
I expect you and the defendant to be here,
ready to go to trial.

280
00:22:38,817 --> 00:22:42,987
In the meantime, the County Detectives'll
determine if his story has any basis in fact.

281
00:22:43,905 --> 00:22:47,659
"Basis in fact"? lf I were brought in dead,
you still wouldn't believe--

282
00:22:47,701 --> 00:22:48,702
Wait a minute.

283
00:22:48,785 --> 00:22:51,663
After all, Your Honor,
would I make up such a story?

284
00:22:51,913 --> 00:22:55,542
That is precisely what were intending
to find out, Mr. Thornhill.

285
00:23:14,894 --> 00:23:16,062
Remember me?

286
00:23:16,187 --> 00:23:17,564
-Yes, sir.
-Good.

287
00:23:17,647 --> 00:23:21,151
-Is Mr. Townsend at home?
-No, I'm sorry he's left for the day.

288
00:23:21,192 --> 00:23:22,444
Mrs. Townsend?

289
00:23:23,194 --> 00:23:26,156
-Who shall I say is calling?
-County Detectives.

290
00:23:26,197 --> 00:23:27,699
Come in, please.

291
00:23:37,709 --> 00:23:39,252
This way, please.

292
00:23:49,679 --> 00:23:51,056
This is the room.

293
00:23:51,139 --> 00:23:52,349
I'll call Madam.

294
00:23:52,390 --> 00:23:53,391
You do that.

295
00:23:53,433 --> 00:23:55,518
Here's the sofa where they held me down.

296
00:23:55,560 --> 00:23:58,855
They spilled bourbon all over it.
I'll show you the stains.

297
00:24:01,566 --> 00:24:03,693
Somebody must've cleaned them off.

298
00:24:04,069 --> 00:24:06,488
This is the cabinet
where they keep the liquor.

299
00:24:06,571 --> 00:24:08,198
Scotch, gin, vodka....

300
00:24:10,158 --> 00:24:11,451
And bourbon.

301
00:24:13,953 --> 00:24:16,456
I remember when it used to come
in bottles.

302
00:24:20,710 --> 00:24:24,089
Dear, we were so worried about you!

303
00:24:24,923 --> 00:24:26,591
Did you get home all right?

304
00:24:26,675 --> 00:24:27,759
Of course you did.

305
00:24:27,842 --> 00:24:31,346
Let me look at you.
A little pink-eyed, but aren't we all?

306
00:24:31,429 --> 00:24:34,099
It was a dull party.
You didn't miss a thing.

307
00:24:34,516 --> 00:24:38,395
I want you all to know,
I never saw this woman before last night.

308
00:24:38,520 --> 00:24:41,606
Mrs. Townsend, I'm Capt. Junket
of the Nassau County Detectives.

309
00:24:41,690 --> 00:24:42,899
This is Lt. Harding.

310
00:24:44,484 --> 00:24:46,861
You haven't gotten into trouble, Roger?

311
00:24:46,945 --> 00:24:49,447
-Stop calling me Roger.
-Has he gotten into trouble?

312
00:24:49,531 --> 00:24:53,952
Mr. Thornhill was picked up last night
driving under the influence of alcohol...

313
00:24:53,993 --> 00:24:55,870
...incidentally, in a stolen car...

314
00:24:55,954 --> 00:24:56,913
Stolen car!

315
00:24:56,955 --> 00:24:59,457
...belonging to Mrs. Babson
of Twining Road.

316
00:24:59,541 --> 00:25:01,960
Roger, you said you were going
to call a cab.

317
00:25:02,002 --> 00:25:04,421
You didn't borrow Laura's Mercedes?

318
00:25:04,629 --> 00:25:06,965
No, I didn't borrow Laura's Mercedes!

319
00:25:07,132 --> 00:25:11,511
Mr. Thornhill has told us that he was
brought here against his will last night...

320
00:25:11,594 --> 00:25:16,433
...and forcibly intoxicated by some friends
of your husband and set out on the road.

321
00:25:16,474 --> 00:25:18,518
Do you know anything about this?

322
00:25:19,144 --> 00:25:22,480
Roger was a bit tipsy
when he arrived here by cab for dinner.

323
00:25:22,564 --> 00:25:23,481
She's lying.

324
00:25:23,523 --> 00:25:26,943
And I'm afraid he became even worse
as the evening wore on.

325
00:25:26,985 --> 00:25:30,238
Finally, he told us he had to go home
to sleep it off.

326
00:25:30,363 --> 00:25:32,824
I knew I should've served dinner earlier.

327
00:25:32,907 --> 00:25:34,367
What a performance!

328
00:25:34,659 --> 00:25:38,163
Mrs. Townsend, does the name
George Kaplan mean anything to you?

329
00:25:38,329 --> 00:25:39,539
George Kaplan?

330
00:25:40,582 --> 00:25:42,167
-No.
-I didn't think so.

331
00:25:43,084 --> 00:25:45,962
Where's her husband?
He's the one you should be questioning.

332
00:25:46,004 --> 00:25:47,797
Is there any place he can be reached?

333
00:25:47,839 --> 00:25:49,674
Yes. The United Nations.

334
00:25:51,801 --> 00:25:54,679
He's addressing the General Assembly
this afternoon.

335
00:25:56,765 --> 00:25:59,309
All right. So he's addressing
the General Assembly.

336
00:25:59,351 --> 00:26:02,187
-Sorry we had to bother you.
-No bother at all.

337
00:26:05,357 --> 00:26:07,067
Now, wait a minute.

338
00:26:12,906 --> 00:26:15,700
Do you want to get in touch
with my husband, Captain?

339
00:26:15,742 --> 00:26:18,078
No, Mrs. Townsend,
that won't be necessary.

340
00:26:18,161 --> 00:26:21,206
Do you mean you're not going
to do any more about this?

341
00:26:22,207 --> 00:26:24,042
Pay the two dollars.

342
00:26:39,849 --> 00:26:40,809
Goodbye.

343
00:27:08,003 --> 00:27:11,756
-I don't see why you want me along.
-You lend a certain air of respectability.

344
00:27:11,840 --> 00:27:13,591
Don't be sarcastic, Roger.

345
00:27:24,978 --> 00:27:27,480
There you are, dear. Park yourself there.

346
00:27:27,897 --> 00:27:29,274
Well, here it goes.

347
00:27:30,358 --> 00:27:31,443
Hello, Operator.

348
00:27:31,526 --> 00:27:34,112
Have you got a George Kaplan
staying here?

349
00:27:35,613 --> 00:27:36,865
That's right.

350
00:27:37,574 --> 00:27:38,575
You have?

351
00:27:38,700 --> 00:27:39,993
Room 796.

352
00:27:41,077 --> 00:27:42,787
Ring it, will you please?

353
00:27:43,121 --> 00:27:44,914
-You see?
-I see.

354
00:27:44,998 --> 00:27:48,793
I hope he clears up this silly business.
You're ruining my whole day.

355
00:27:50,962 --> 00:27:53,173
Did he leave word when he'd be back?

356
00:27:54,341 --> 00:27:55,467
Really?

357
00:27:55,800 --> 00:27:56,926
Thank you.

358
00:27:58,011 --> 00:28:01,097
That's odd. He hasn't answered
his telephone in two days.

359
00:28:01,181 --> 00:28:03,141
Maybe he got locked in the bathroom.

360
00:28:03,183 --> 00:28:05,185
Mother, do me a favor, will you?

361
00:28:05,310 --> 00:28:09,773
Put on that innocent look you do so well
and go to the desk and get the key to 796.

362
00:28:09,856 --> 00:28:11,649
I wouldn't do such a thing!

363
00:28:11,733 --> 00:28:14,027
-$10?
-Not for all the money in the world.

364
00:28:14,152 --> 00:28:16,571
-$50?
-Roger, you are disgraceful.

365
00:28:21,326 --> 00:28:25,455
Car theft, drunk driving,
assaulting an officer, lying to a judge...

366
00:28:25,872 --> 00:28:27,415
...and now, house-breaking.

367
00:28:27,499 --> 00:28:30,794
-No, hotel-breaking. There's a difference.
-Of five to ten years.

368
00:28:30,835 --> 00:28:32,212
Just a minute, please.

369
00:28:34,839 --> 00:28:37,467
Will you be wanting me
to change your bedding, sir?

370
00:28:37,509 --> 00:28:39,511
Yes. Well, but not right now.

371
00:28:39,594 --> 00:28:42,889
I mention it, because the bed
doesn't seem like it's been slept in...

372
00:28:42,972 --> 00:28:46,017
...and I was wondering
if I ought to change the linens.

373
00:28:46,142 --> 00:28:47,977
Thank you for your interest.

374
00:28:49,521 --> 00:28:50,522
Come on.

375
00:28:54,192 --> 00:28:56,319
She seemed to think I'm Kaplan.

376
00:28:57,195 --> 00:28:59,155
I wonder if I look like Kaplan.

377
00:29:10,083 --> 00:29:12,043
-Look who's here.
-Who? Where?

378
00:29:12,377 --> 00:29:15,922
Our friend who's assembling
the General Assembly this afternoon.

379
00:29:16,381 --> 00:29:18,216
Roger, I think we should go.

380
00:29:18,550 --> 00:29:19,592
Don't be nervous.

381
00:29:19,676 --> 00:29:23,722
-I'm not. I'll be late for the bridge club.
-Good. You'll lose less than usual.

382
00:29:38,778 --> 00:29:41,489
Bulletin, Kaplan has dandruff.

383
00:29:41,573 --> 00:29:43,533
In that case, I think we should leave.

384
00:29:43,575 --> 00:29:44,743
Too late.

385
00:29:50,248 --> 00:29:53,251
-You rang for me, sir?
-Yes. Come in for a moment.

386
00:29:54,294 --> 00:29:56,546
-What's your name?
-Elsie, sir.

387
00:29:59,132 --> 00:30:01,801
-Do you know who I am?
-You're Mr. Kaplan.

388
00:30:02,635 --> 00:30:05,138
When did you first see me?

389
00:30:05,513 --> 00:30:06,598
Outside the door.

390
00:30:07,265 --> 00:30:10,393
Out in the hall a couple of minutes ago.
Don't you remember?

391
00:30:10,435 --> 00:30:12,645
Is that the first time you laid eyes on me?

392
00:30:12,729 --> 00:30:15,148
Can I help it if you're never around,
Mr. Kaplan?

393
00:30:15,231 --> 00:30:17,233
Then how do you know I'm Mr. Kaplan?

394
00:30:17,317 --> 00:30:19,486
-What?
-How do you know I'm Mr. Kaplan?

395
00:30:19,569 --> 00:30:20,987
Of course you are.

396
00:30:21,071 --> 00:30:22,822
This is Room 796, isn't it?

397
00:30:23,114 --> 00:30:25,950
So, you're the gentleman in Room 796,
aren't you?

398
00:30:27,035 --> 00:30:28,453
All right, Elsie, thanks.

399
00:30:28,536 --> 00:30:31,331
-Will that be all, sir?
-For the time being, yes.

400
00:30:37,253 --> 00:30:38,254
Valet.

401
00:30:38,380 --> 00:30:39,589
Yes, come in.

402
00:30:40,799 --> 00:30:43,927
-Should I hang it in the closet, Mr. Kaplan?
-Yes, please.

403
00:30:50,225 --> 00:30:51,351
Tell me....

404
00:30:51,643 --> 00:30:54,020
What time did I give you that suit?

405
00:30:55,146 --> 00:30:57,148
Last night. Around 6:00.

406
00:30:58,191 --> 00:31:00,819
-Did I give it to you personally?
-Personally?

407
00:31:00,902 --> 00:31:04,447
No. You called down on the phone
and described the suit to me.

408
00:31:04,531 --> 00:31:07,826
Said it would be hanging in your closet.
Like you always do.

409
00:31:08,201 --> 00:31:10,620
-Anything wrong?
-No, I'm just curious.

410
00:31:11,079 --> 00:31:12,831
-Here. Thanks.
-Thank you.

411
00:31:13,248 --> 00:31:15,166
Nice to meet you, Mr. Kaplan.

412
00:31:19,587 --> 00:31:21,423
Isn't that the damnedest thing?

413
00:31:21,506 --> 00:31:25,010
I'm beginning to think that no one
in the hotel has actually seen Kaplan.

414
00:31:25,093 --> 00:31:28,096
Maybe he has his suits mended
by invisible weavers.

415
00:31:34,269 --> 00:31:35,812
Let me see something.

416
00:31:42,235 --> 00:31:44,696
I don't think that one does
anything for you.

417
00:31:49,993 --> 00:31:51,411
Now, that's much better.

418
00:31:52,245 --> 00:31:55,540
Obviously they've mistaken me
for a much shorter man.

419
00:31:59,878 --> 00:32:01,796
-Should l?
-Certainly not.

420
00:32:03,673 --> 00:32:05,008
Hold that.

421
00:32:09,763 --> 00:32:12,265
It's good to find you in, Mr. Kaplan.

422
00:32:12,640 --> 00:32:13,850
Who is this?

423
00:32:13,892 --> 00:32:17,103
We met only last night and still
you do not recognize my voice.

424
00:32:17,187 --> 00:32:19,105
I should feel offended.

425
00:32:19,356 --> 00:32:22,567
Yes, I know who you are
and I'm not Mr. Kaplan.

426
00:32:22,901 --> 00:32:26,529
Of course not. You answer his telephone,
you live in his hotel room...

427
00:32:26,613 --> 00:32:28,782
...and yet you are not Mr. Kaplan.

428
00:32:28,865 --> 00:32:31,576
Nevertheless,
we are pleased to find you in.

429
00:32:32,577 --> 00:32:33,787
Now wait....

430
00:32:35,288 --> 00:32:36,581
Operator?

431
00:32:37,332 --> 00:32:40,585
Operator, this is Mr. Thorn....
Kaplan in 796.

432
00:32:40,919 --> 00:32:44,547
Where did that call come from?
From the outside or from the lobby?

433
00:32:44,589 --> 00:32:47,092
-Just a minute. I'll see.
-Hurry, please.

434
00:32:47,133 --> 00:32:48,134
Who was it?

435
00:32:48,259 --> 00:32:50,595
One of the men
who tried to kill me last night.

436
00:32:50,637 --> 00:32:52,305
We're back to that one, are we?

437
00:32:54,474 --> 00:32:57,852
-That call was made from the lobby, sir.
-It was?

438
00:32:58,561 --> 00:33:00,730
They're probably on their way up here.

439
00:33:00,814 --> 00:33:04,359
-Let's get out of here.
-I think I'd like to meet these killers.

440
00:33:48,403 --> 00:33:51,656
You gentlemen aren't really trying
to kill my son, are you?

441
00:34:14,888 --> 00:34:16,931
Lobby, please. Watch your step.

442
00:34:17,849 --> 00:34:21,227
Gentlemen, please. Ladies first.
Come along, ladies.

443
00:34:21,311 --> 00:34:23,271
That's right. Come along. Good.

444
00:34:24,773 --> 00:34:26,733
Roger, will you be home for dinner?

445
00:34:30,695 --> 00:34:33,281
-Where to?
-I don't know. Just keep going.

446
00:34:49,214 --> 00:34:51,841
-Take me to the United Nations.
-Right.

447
00:34:51,925 --> 00:34:53,635
General Assembly Building.

448
00:34:53,718 --> 00:34:54,719
Right.

449
00:34:54,803 --> 00:34:57,722
-I'm being followed. Can you do anything?
-Yes, I can.

450
00:34:57,764 --> 00:34:58,306
Do it.

451
00:35:31,589 --> 00:35:33,216
-May I help you?
-Yes, please.

452
00:35:33,299 --> 00:35:35,427
Where will I find Mr. Lester Townsend?

453
00:35:35,510 --> 00:35:37,762
Mr. Lester Townsend of UNIPO?

454
00:35:38,138 --> 00:35:40,432
Did you have an appointment, sir?

455
00:35:40,974 --> 00:35:42,892
Well, yes. He expects me.

456
00:35:42,934 --> 00:35:44,519
Your name, please?

457
00:35:44,602 --> 00:35:46,229
-My name?
-Yes, please.

458
00:35:46,938 --> 00:35:48,523
Kaplan. George Kaplan.

459
00:35:50,900 --> 00:35:52,569
One moment, please.

460
00:35:58,533 --> 00:36:00,618
Wait for me at the corner of 47th.

461
00:36:07,959 --> 00:36:12,088
If you'd give this to one of the attendants
in the public lounge, she'll page him.

462
00:36:12,172 --> 00:36:15,008
-Thank you very much.
-You're welcome, Mr. Kaplan.

463
00:36:37,489 --> 00:36:39,616
Page Mr. Lester Townsend, please?

464
00:36:39,699 --> 00:36:41,576
Certainly, Mr. Kaplan.

465
00:36:41,826 --> 00:36:43,453
Mr. Townsend of UNIPO.

466
00:36:43,536 --> 00:36:45,413
Mr. Townsend of UNIPO.

467
00:36:45,538 --> 00:36:49,209
Please call at the communications desk
of the Public Lounge.

468
00:36:58,176 --> 00:36:59,386
You paged me?

469
00:36:59,636 --> 00:37:01,012
-Mr. Kaplan?
-Yes.

470
00:37:01,054 --> 00:37:04,265
You wanted to see Mr. Townsend.
This is Mr. Townsend.

471
00:37:04,349 --> 00:37:06,184
How do you do, Mr. Kaplan?

472
00:37:06,643 --> 00:37:08,436
This isn't Mr. Townsend.

473
00:37:08,561 --> 00:37:09,854
Yes, it is.

474
00:37:10,855 --> 00:37:13,400
There must be some mistake.
Mr. Lester Townsend?

475
00:37:13,483 --> 00:37:15,985
That's me. What can I do for you?

476
00:37:19,864 --> 00:37:22,033
Are you the Townsend
who lives in Glen Cove?

477
00:37:22,117 --> 00:37:24,369
That's right. Are we neighbors?

478
00:37:25,328 --> 00:37:28,331
A large, red-brick house
with a curved, tree-lined driveway?

479
00:37:28,373 --> 00:37:29,874
That's the one.

480
00:37:30,875 --> 00:37:32,752
Were you at home last night?

481
00:37:32,836 --> 00:37:34,629
-You mean in Glen Cove?
-Yes.

482
00:37:34,713 --> 00:37:37,549
I've been staying in my apartment
in town for the last month.

483
00:37:37,590 --> 00:37:39,968
I always do when we're in session here.

484
00:37:40,051 --> 00:37:41,469
What about Mrs. Townsend?

485
00:37:41,970 --> 00:37:44,431
My wife has been dead for many years.

486
00:37:44,556 --> 00:37:46,683
Mr. Kaplan, what's this all about?

487
00:37:47,183 --> 00:37:49,394
Who are those people living in your house?

488
00:37:49,477 --> 00:37:51,938
What people?
The house is completely closed up.

489
00:37:52,397 --> 00:37:55,358
There's just a gardener and his wife
living on the grounds.

490
00:37:55,400 --> 00:37:58,069
Now, Mr. Kaplan, tell me who you are
and what you want.

491
00:38:03,241 --> 00:38:04,868
Do you know this man?

492
00:38:13,168 --> 00:38:14,169
Look!

493
00:38:18,882 --> 00:38:20,383
He's got a knife! Look out!

494
00:38:20,467 --> 00:38:23,011
Listen to me.
I had nothing to do with this.

495
00:38:23,303 --> 00:38:25,722
-Call the police.
-Don't come any nearer!

496
00:38:25,889 --> 00:38:26,890
Get back!

497
00:38:42,864 --> 00:38:45,909
UNITED STATES INTELLIGENCE AGENCY

498
00:38:46,326 --> 00:38:49,662
"The photograph has been identified
as that of Roger Thornhill...

499
00:38:49,788 --> 00:38:54,501
"...a Manhattan advertising executive,
indicating that the name George Kaplan...

500
00:38:54,626 --> 00:38:57,921
"...which he gave to an attendant
in the General Assembly Building...

501
00:38:58,004 --> 00:38:59,214
"...was false.

502
00:39:00,006 --> 00:39:03,635
"A possible motive for the slaying
was suggested by the discovery...

503
00:39:03,927 --> 00:39:07,806
"...that earlier today, Thornhill appeared
in a Glen Cove police court...

504
00:39:07,806 --> 00:39:10,934
"...charged with drunk driving
with a stolen car...

505
00:39:12,018 --> 00:39:15,522
"...and in his defense he charged
that the murder victim, Mr. Townsend...

506
00:39:15,605 --> 00:39:18,191
"...had tried to kill him the night before."

507
00:39:18,358 --> 00:39:19,317
Brother!

508
00:39:19,609 --> 00:39:21,027
What about that?

509
00:39:21,861 --> 00:39:24,197
Does anyone know this Thornhill?

510
00:39:24,739 --> 00:39:25,824
No, not me.

511
00:39:25,907 --> 00:39:27,158
Never heard of him.

512
00:39:27,659 --> 00:39:28,743
Professor?

513
00:39:28,910 --> 00:39:32,497
Apparently, the poor sucker got mistaken
for George Kaplan.

514
00:39:32,914 --> 00:39:36,292
How could he be mistaken for
George Kaplan when he doesn't even exist?

515
00:39:36,376 --> 00:39:39,671
Don't ask me how it happened,
but obviously it happened.

516
00:39:39,713 --> 00:39:43,550
Vandamm's men must have grabbed him
and tried to put him away...

517
00:39:43,717 --> 00:39:45,635
...using Lester Townsend's house.

518
00:39:45,719 --> 00:39:49,055
And the unsuspecting Townsend winds up
with a stray knife in his back.

519
00:39:50,223 --> 00:39:51,766
C'est la guerre.

520
00:39:52,058 --> 00:39:54,686
It's so horribly sad.
Why is it I feel like laughing?

521
00:39:54,769 --> 00:39:56,479
What are we going to do?

522
00:39:56,563 --> 00:39:57,439
Do?

523
00:39:57,731 --> 00:39:59,524
About Mr. Thornhill?

524
00:40:00,191 --> 00:40:02,694
We...do nothing!

525
00:40:03,820 --> 00:40:04,529
Nothing?

526
00:40:04,904 --> 00:40:06,489
That's right. Nothing.

527
00:40:07,115 --> 00:40:11,202
We could congratulate ourselves
on a marvelous stroke of good fortune.

528
00:40:12,412 --> 00:40:16,541
Our non-existent decoy, George Kaplan,
created to divert suspicion...

529
00:40:16,583 --> 00:40:20,545
...from our actual agent,
has fortuitously become a live decoy.

530
00:40:20,628 --> 00:40:22,088
Yes, Professor.

531
00:40:22,172 --> 00:40:24,549
How long do you think he'll stay alive?

532
00:40:25,508 --> 00:40:27,135
That's his problem.

533
00:40:27,260 --> 00:40:28,928
What Mrs. Finley means--

534
00:40:29,137 --> 00:40:30,430
I know what she means.

535
00:40:30,513 --> 00:40:33,725
We can't sit back calmly and wait to see
who kills him first!

536
00:40:33,767 --> 00:40:35,769
Vandamm and company or the police?

537
00:40:37,103 --> 00:40:40,565
What can we do to save him
without endangering our own agent?

538
00:40:40,899 --> 00:40:43,401
Aren't we being just a wee bit callous?

539
00:40:43,651 --> 00:40:46,237
No, my dear woman,
we're not being callous.

540
00:40:46,654 --> 00:40:50,825
We didn't invent our non-existent man
and give him the name of George Kaplan...

541
00:40:50,909 --> 00:40:53,912
...establish elaborate behavior patterns
for him...

542
00:40:53,995 --> 00:40:58,583
...move his prop belongings in and out
of hotel rooms, for our private amusement.

543
00:40:59,459 --> 00:41:01,252
We created George Kaplan...

544
00:41:01,378 --> 00:41:03,880
...and labored successfully
to convince Vandamm...

545
00:41:03,963 --> 00:41:07,926
...that this was our agent hot on his trail
for a desperately important reason.

546
00:41:08,677 --> 00:41:10,261
Nobody's denying that!

547
00:41:10,470 --> 00:41:11,638
Very well, then.

548
00:41:11,721 --> 00:41:16,226
If we make the slightest move to suggest
there's no such agent as George Kaplan...

549
00:41:16,309 --> 00:41:20,772
...give any hint to Vandamm he's pursuing
a decoy instead of our own agent...

550
00:41:20,814 --> 00:41:24,192
...then our agent, working right under
Vandamm's very nose...

551
00:41:24,776 --> 00:41:27,445
...will immediately face suspicion,
exposure...

552
00:41:28,113 --> 00:41:29,739
...and assassination.

553
00:41:31,032 --> 00:41:32,909
Like the two others who went before.

554
00:41:33,201 --> 00:41:36,329
Goodbye, Mr. Thornhill, wherever you are.

555
00:41:44,921 --> 00:41:47,215
Train number 25...

556
00:41:47,799 --> 00:41:50,927
...the 20th Century Limited...

557
00:41:51,803 --> 00:41:56,141
...due to leave in 6:00 p.m. for Chicago....

558
00:42:01,187 --> 00:42:02,689
Yes, dear. I know.

559
00:42:03,148 --> 00:42:06,276
Now, listen to me, Mother,
I called the Plaza.

560
00:42:06,693 --> 00:42:09,946
Kaplan checked out and went
to the Ambassador East in Chicago.

561
00:42:10,030 --> 00:42:11,448
That's why I'm--

562
00:42:12,198 --> 00:42:14,826
I can't go to the police. At least not yet.

563
00:42:14,951 --> 00:42:17,746
You saw the newspapers.
My fingerprints are on the knife.

564
00:42:17,829 --> 00:42:20,915
I'm a car thief, a drunk driver,
and a murderer.

565
00:42:21,082 --> 00:42:22,584
I wouldn't have a chance.

566
00:42:22,667 --> 00:42:27,339
I won't have, until I find George Kaplan
who obviously knows what this is about.

567
00:42:27,505 --> 00:42:29,507
No, the train. It's safer.

568
00:42:30,175 --> 00:42:34,137
Because there's no place to hide on a plane
if anyone should recognize me.

569
00:42:34,971 --> 00:42:37,390
You want me to jump off a moving plane?

570
00:42:38,683 --> 00:42:41,353
Well, thank you so much, Mother.
Goodbye.

571
00:42:55,241 --> 00:42:57,035
MANHUNT ON FOR U. N. KILLER

572
00:43:20,433 --> 00:43:22,977
Give me a bedroom
on the 20th Century, please.

573
00:43:23,061 --> 00:43:24,604
It's leaving in five minutes.

574
00:43:24,688 --> 00:43:26,439
I know. Will you make it snappy?

575
00:43:26,523 --> 00:43:28,441
I think they're all sold out.

576
00:43:28,608 --> 00:43:29,526
Sold out?

577
00:43:29,609 --> 00:43:31,277
You can always go coach.

578
00:43:31,444 --> 00:43:34,114
No, I can't do that.
What time is the next train?

579
00:43:34,197 --> 00:43:37,158
Nothing till 10:00. You're in a hurry, huh?

580
00:43:37,867 --> 00:43:40,412
Could you call them
and see what they have?

581
00:43:40,453 --> 00:43:42,122
Something wrong with your eyes?

582
00:43:42,205 --> 00:43:45,333
Yes, they're sensitive to questions.
Will you call them?

583
00:43:45,500 --> 00:43:46,835
Sure. Sure.

584
00:43:50,005 --> 00:43:51,006
Don't go away!

585
00:44:00,515 --> 00:44:03,226
He's at Window 15, upper level. Hurry.

586
00:44:05,186 --> 00:44:06,938
You're in luck, mister.

587
00:44:14,279 --> 00:44:16,781
-Ticket?
-I'm just seeing some friends off.

588
00:44:18,575 --> 00:44:20,118
I have to know their names.

589
00:44:21,202 --> 00:44:22,370
Come here!

590
00:44:54,986 --> 00:44:55,820
I'm sorry.

591
00:44:55,862 --> 00:44:57,655
-I'm sorry.
-My fault.

592
00:44:58,698 --> 00:44:59,699
Sorry.

593
00:45:09,292 --> 00:45:11,378
He went that way. I think he got off.

594
00:45:12,128 --> 00:45:13,630
All aboard!

595
00:45:17,425 --> 00:45:19,719
-Thank you.
-Quite all right.

596
00:45:19,761 --> 00:45:21,638
Seven parking tickets.

597
00:46:41,426 --> 00:46:43,636
-Good evening, sir. One?
-Please.

598
00:46:57,776 --> 00:46:59,194
A cocktail before dinner?

599
00:46:59,277 --> 00:47:01,488
-Yes, please. A Gibson.
-Right away.

600
00:47:11,456 --> 00:47:12,624
Here we are again.

601
00:47:13,458 --> 00:47:14,376
Yes.

602
00:47:16,378 --> 00:47:17,712
You recommend anything?

603
00:47:17,796 --> 00:47:21,466
The brook trout.
A little "trouty" but quite good.

604
00:47:22,342 --> 00:47:23,301
Sold.

605
00:47:25,512 --> 00:47:26,805
"Brook...

606
00:47:27,055 --> 00:47:28,098
"...trout."

607
00:47:28,682 --> 00:47:30,392
-There you are.
-Yes, sir.

608
00:47:42,696 --> 00:47:44,572
I know. I look vaguely familiar.

609
00:47:44,656 --> 00:47:45,573
Yes.

610
00:47:46,032 --> 00:47:48,410
You feel you've seen me
somewhere before.

611
00:47:50,286 --> 00:47:53,123
I have that effect on people.
It's something about my face.

612
00:47:54,457 --> 00:47:56,042
It's a nice face.

613
00:47:57,002 --> 00:47:59,963
-You think so?
-I wouldn't say it if I didn't.

614
00:48:00,839 --> 00:48:02,632
Oh, you're that type.

615
00:48:02,716 --> 00:48:03,800
What type?

616
00:48:04,175 --> 00:48:05,218
Honest.

617
00:48:05,885 --> 00:48:06,886
Not really.

618
00:48:07,637 --> 00:48:10,015
Good. Because honest women
frighten me.

619
00:48:10,098 --> 00:48:11,057
Why?

620
00:48:11,766 --> 00:48:14,394
Somehow they seem to put me
at a disadvantage.

621
00:48:14,686 --> 00:48:17,772
-Because you're not honest with them?
-Exactly.

622
00:48:18,023 --> 00:48:21,192
Like that business about
the seven parking tickets?

623
00:48:21,276 --> 00:48:24,529
What I mean is,
the moment I meet an attractive woman...

624
00:48:25,905 --> 00:48:29,451
...l have to start pretending I've no desire
to make love to her.

625
00:48:29,784 --> 00:48:31,953
What makes you think
you have to conceal it?

626
00:48:32,620 --> 00:48:34,581
She might find the idea objectionable.

627
00:48:35,290 --> 00:48:37,167
Then again, she might not.

628
00:48:40,503 --> 00:48:42,922
Think how lucky I am
to have been seated here.

629
00:48:43,006 --> 00:48:45,091
Luck had nothing to do with it.

630
00:48:45,675 --> 00:48:46,343
Fate?

631
00:48:48,636 --> 00:48:52,223
I tipped the steward $5 to seat you here
if you should come in.

632
00:48:56,728 --> 00:48:58,021
Is that a proposition?

633
00:48:59,731 --> 00:49:01,983
I never discuss love on an empty stomach.

634
00:49:02,942 --> 00:49:04,194
You've already eaten.

635
00:49:04,944 --> 00:49:06,029
But you haven't.

636
00:49:13,703 --> 00:49:15,914
Don't you think it's time
we were introduced?

637
00:49:16,373 --> 00:49:19,584
I'm Eve Kendall. I'm 26 and unmarried.

638
00:49:20,794 --> 00:49:22,504
Now you know everything.

639
00:49:23,254 --> 00:49:26,925
What do you do besides lure men
to their doom on the 20th Century Limited?

640
00:49:27,175 --> 00:49:28,968
I'm an industrial designer.

641
00:49:30,887 --> 00:49:32,013
Jack Phillips.

642
00:49:32,097 --> 00:49:34,974
Western sales manager
for Kingby Electronics.

643
00:49:35,141 --> 00:49:37,519
No, you're not. You're Roger Thornhill...

644
00:49:37,936 --> 00:49:39,312
...of Madison Avenue...

645
00:49:39,354 --> 00:49:42,440
...and you're wanted for murder
on every front page in America.

646
00:49:42,857 --> 00:49:44,567
Don't be so modest.

647
00:49:47,654 --> 00:49:48,571
Don't worry.

648
00:49:49,114 --> 00:49:51,199
-I won't say a word.
-How come?

649
00:49:52,534 --> 00:49:53,910
I told you.

650
00:49:54,494 --> 00:49:55,537
It's a nice face.

651
00:49:56,079 --> 00:49:57,497
Is that the only reason?

652
00:49:58,039 --> 00:49:59,624
It's going to be a long night.

653
00:50:01,251 --> 00:50:02,168
True.

654
00:50:02,460 --> 00:50:04,921
I don't particularly like the book
I've started.

655
00:50:06,840 --> 00:50:08,049
You know what I mean?

656
00:50:09,551 --> 00:50:10,969
Let me think.

657
00:50:15,015 --> 00:50:16,641
I know exactly what you mean.

658
00:50:26,526 --> 00:50:27,986
R.O.T.

659
00:50:29,487 --> 00:50:31,156
That's my trademark. R.O.T.

660
00:50:32,032 --> 00:50:34,409
Roger O. Thornhill.
What does the "O" stand for?

661
00:50:35,535 --> 00:50:36,661
Nothing.

662
00:51:00,018 --> 00:51:02,854
I'd invite you to my bedroom
if I had a bedroom.

663
00:51:03,438 --> 00:51:04,314
A roomette?

664
00:51:04,397 --> 00:51:05,899
Nothing, not even a ticket.

665
00:51:06,066 --> 00:51:09,778
I've been playing hide-and-seek
ever since the train left New York.

666
00:51:10,820 --> 00:51:12,072
How awkward for you.

667
00:51:12,238 --> 00:51:13,239
Yes, isn't it?

668
00:51:13,406 --> 00:51:14,532
No place to sleep.

669
00:51:16,076 --> 00:51:18,244
I have a large drawing-room all to myself.

670
00:51:19,079 --> 00:51:20,705
That doesn't seem quite fair.

671
00:51:21,915 --> 00:51:23,249
Drawing-room E.

672
00:51:24,084 --> 00:51:25,835
Car 3901 .

673
00:51:26,795 --> 00:51:28,171
Such a nice number.

674
00:51:29,756 --> 00:51:31,591
It's easy to remember.

675
00:51:31,716 --> 00:51:33,176
3901 .

676
00:51:34,511 --> 00:51:35,345
See?

677
00:51:36,930 --> 00:51:37,889
No luggage.

678
00:51:41,559 --> 00:51:42,394
So?

679
00:51:42,602 --> 00:51:45,730
You wouldn't happen to have
an extra pair of pajamas, would you?

680
00:51:45,980 --> 00:51:47,107
Wouldn't l?

681
00:51:49,192 --> 00:51:51,444
I wouldn't order any dessert if I were you.

682
00:51:52,487 --> 00:51:53,655
I get the message.

683
00:51:54,572 --> 00:51:56,241
That isn't exactly what I meant.

684
00:51:56,950 --> 00:51:59,452
The train seems to be making
an unscheduled stop.

685
00:51:59,536 --> 00:52:03,081
I saw two men get out of a police car
as we pulled into the station.

686
00:52:03,790 --> 00:52:05,041
They weren't smiling.

687
00:52:27,397 --> 00:52:30,150
You better go out
and tell those police to hurry.

688
00:52:30,233 --> 00:52:31,818
Patience is a virtue.

689
00:52:31,943 --> 00:52:33,445
So is breathing.

690
00:52:33,570 --> 00:52:35,071
Just lie still.

691
00:52:36,740 --> 00:52:38,533
Have you got any olive oil?

692
00:52:38,616 --> 00:52:39,826
Olive oil?

693
00:52:40,035 --> 00:52:43,038
I want to be packed in olive oil
if I'm going to be a sardine.

694
00:52:44,789 --> 00:52:45,582
Come in.

695
00:52:47,417 --> 00:52:49,627
-Who are you?
-State Police.

696
00:52:50,837 --> 00:52:52,339
Your name please?

697
00:52:52,380 --> 00:52:54,799
Eve Kendall. Is anything wrong?

698
00:52:54,966 --> 00:52:57,552
There was a man at your table tonight
in the dining car.

699
00:52:58,345 --> 00:52:59,304
Yes.

700
00:52:59,471 --> 00:53:00,972
Friend of yours?

701
00:53:01,514 --> 00:53:02,891
I never saw him before.

702
00:53:04,267 --> 00:53:05,643
Is this the man?

703
00:53:07,145 --> 00:53:10,106
Yes, I think so.
It's not a very clear picture.

704
00:53:10,523 --> 00:53:13,568
It's a wire-photo.
We just got it from the New York police.

705
00:53:13,651 --> 00:53:14,527
Police?

706
00:53:14,611 --> 00:53:15,945
He's wanted for murder.

707
00:53:17,238 --> 00:53:18,865
Good heavens, no!

708
00:53:19,366 --> 00:53:21,826
The steward said
you left the dining car together.

709
00:53:22,452 --> 00:53:26,039
We might have happened to leave
at the same time but not together.

710
00:53:26,873 --> 00:53:28,375
What did you two talk about?

711
00:53:29,417 --> 00:53:30,377
Talk about?

712
00:53:30,418 --> 00:53:33,505
The waiter said you were getting along fine
with this Thornhill.

713
00:53:33,630 --> 00:53:35,256
Is that his name? Thornhill?

714
00:53:36,633 --> 00:53:37,967
He didn't tell you?

715
00:53:39,052 --> 00:53:41,179
He didn't tell me anything.

716
00:53:41,388 --> 00:53:44,683
All we did was chat about
different kinds of food...

717
00:53:44,724 --> 00:53:47,852
...train travel versus plane travel,
that sort of thing.

718
00:53:48,395 --> 00:53:52,357
Rather innocuous, I must say,
considering he was a fugitive from justice.

719
00:53:52,899 --> 00:53:53,983
Who did he kill?

720
00:53:54,109 --> 00:53:55,902
He didn't say where he was going?

721
00:53:56,903 --> 00:53:57,904
No.

722
00:53:58,029 --> 00:53:59,656
I assumed Chicago.

723
00:54:00,073 --> 00:54:02,117
Perhaps he got off when you got on?

724
00:54:03,410 --> 00:54:05,578
If you happen to see him again, Miss...

725
00:54:05,995 --> 00:54:06,996
Kendall.

726
00:54:07,163 --> 00:54:08,873
...will you let us know?

727
00:54:09,499 --> 00:54:14,045
I'm going to bed and I'll lock my door.
I doubt if I'll see anybody else tonight.

728
00:54:14,254 --> 00:54:17,757
In case you do, we'll be in
the observation car at the rear of the train.

729
00:54:18,717 --> 00:54:20,260
It's comforting to know that.

730
00:54:21,803 --> 00:54:22,887
Good night.

731
00:54:24,931 --> 00:54:26,349
Still breathing?

732
00:54:26,433 --> 00:54:28,727
Either hurry up or get me a snorkel.

733
00:54:29,769 --> 00:54:33,231
I'm looking for the can opener
I stole from the porter.

734
00:54:38,486 --> 00:54:39,904
Hello, there.

735
00:54:40,196 --> 00:54:41,239
Hello.

736
00:54:53,752 --> 00:54:55,962
Tell me, why are you so good to me?

737
00:54:57,297 --> 00:54:59,299
Shall I climb up and tell you why?

738
00:55:13,480 --> 00:55:14,939
I've been thinking...

739
00:55:15,607 --> 00:55:19,611
...it's not safe for you to roam Chicago
looking for this George Kaplan...

740
00:55:19,694 --> 00:55:21,738
...you've been telling me about.

741
00:55:22,197 --> 00:55:25,784
You'll be picked up by the police
the moment you show your face.

742
00:55:26,242 --> 00:55:27,911
It's such a nice face, too.

743
00:55:30,705 --> 00:55:35,585
Don't you think it'd be a better idea
if you stayed in my hotel room...

744
00:55:36,086 --> 00:55:39,673
...while I located him for you
and brought him to you?

745
00:55:40,507 --> 00:55:43,176
I can't let you get involved.
It's too dangerous.

746
00:55:44,302 --> 00:55:45,679
I'm a big girl.

747
00:55:47,055 --> 00:55:49,557
Yeah, and in all the right places, too.

748
00:55:54,688 --> 00:55:57,565
You know, this is ridiculous.
You know that, don't you?

749
00:55:57,649 --> 00:55:58,566
Yes.

750
00:55:58,900 --> 00:56:00,944
I mean, we've hardly met.

751
00:56:01,152 --> 00:56:02,362
That's right.

752
00:56:03,113 --> 00:56:05,323
How do I know you aren't a murderer?

753
00:56:05,865 --> 00:56:07,117
You don't.

754
00:56:07,701 --> 00:56:11,204
Maybe you're planning to murder me,
right here, tonight.

755
00:56:12,122 --> 00:56:13,248
Shall l?

756
00:56:14,541 --> 00:56:15,792
Please do.

757
00:56:36,646 --> 00:56:38,356
Beats flying, doesn't it?

758
00:56:40,817 --> 00:56:42,318
We should stop.

759
00:56:42,652 --> 00:56:43,987
Immediately.

760
00:56:44,571 --> 00:56:46,573
I ought to know more about you.

761
00:56:47,323 --> 00:56:48,908
What more could you know?

762
00:56:51,786 --> 00:56:54,039
You're an advertising man,
that's all I know.

763
00:56:54,622 --> 00:56:55,874
That's right.

764
00:56:58,460 --> 00:57:00,712
The train's a little unsteady.

765
00:57:00,754 --> 00:57:02,005
Who isn't?

766
00:57:02,922 --> 00:57:04,758
What else do you know?

767
00:57:06,134 --> 00:57:09,387
You've got taste in clothes, taste in food....

768
00:57:12,265 --> 00:57:15,143
And taste in women. I like your flavor.

769
00:57:23,651 --> 00:57:25,779
You're very clever with words.

770
00:57:26,112 --> 00:57:28,782
You can probably make them
do anything for you.

771
00:57:29,949 --> 00:57:32,202
Sell people things they don't need...

772
00:57:32,369 --> 00:57:35,413
...make women who don't know you
fall in love with you.

773
00:57:37,916 --> 00:57:40,585
I'm beginning to think I'm underpaid.

774
00:57:54,265 --> 00:57:55,475
Look out.

775
00:58:07,112 --> 00:58:09,906
-Don't bother with the washroom.
-Yes, ma'am.

776
00:58:09,989 --> 00:58:12,909
By the way, I found this on the floor.
Does it belong to you?

777
00:58:12,992 --> 00:58:15,120
Yes, ma'am. I've been looking for it.

778
00:58:15,161 --> 00:58:17,330
-I'll wait outside.
-Thank you.

779
00:58:34,389 --> 00:58:35,473
Thank you, Porter.

780
00:58:35,849 --> 00:58:37,684
Thank you, ma'am. Good night now.

781
00:58:37,767 --> 00:58:39,019
Good night.

782
00:58:43,398 --> 00:58:45,692
Come out, come out, wherever you are.

783
00:58:50,739 --> 00:58:51,865
The porter.

784
00:58:54,117 --> 00:58:55,368
So I see.

785
00:59:03,835 --> 00:59:05,253
Where were we?

786
00:59:05,378 --> 00:59:06,504
Here.

787
00:59:11,968 --> 00:59:13,970
Nice of him to have opened the bed.

788
00:59:16,097 --> 00:59:17,390
Only one bed.

789
00:59:19,768 --> 00:59:22,103
That's a good omen, don't you think?

790
00:59:22,187 --> 00:59:23,396
Wonderful.

791
00:59:24,856 --> 00:59:26,691
Do you know what that means?

792
00:59:27,525 --> 00:59:28,818
What? Tell me.

793
00:59:29,903 --> 00:59:32,655
It means you'll sleep on the floor.

794
00:59:54,427 --> 00:59:56,429
A message from the lady in 3901 .

795
01:00:01,726 --> 01:00:05,730
What do I do with him
in the morning? Eve.

796
01:00:40,515 --> 01:00:43,018
-You keep walking. I'll catch up.
-Yes, ma'am.

797
01:00:44,644 --> 01:00:45,979
Anything to report?

798
01:00:46,980 --> 01:00:48,857
Yes. I had a fine night's sleep.

799
01:00:49,649 --> 01:00:52,068
I mean, have you seen the man
we're looking for?

800
01:00:52,777 --> 01:00:53,945
Mr. Thornycroft?

801
01:00:54,904 --> 01:00:55,822
Thornhill.

802
01:00:57,532 --> 01:00:59,492
No, no. I'm awfully sorry.

803
01:01:00,035 --> 01:01:01,828
But good luck to you both.

804
01:01:06,416 --> 01:01:09,669
-How are we doing?
-I may collapse at any moment.

805
01:01:10,128 --> 01:01:12,964
Not yet. First we have to run the gauntlet.
Look.

806
01:01:18,845 --> 01:01:22,766
I'm accustomed to having a load on,
but what have you got in these bags?

807
01:01:22,849 --> 01:01:25,393
-Bowling balls, naturally.
-Oh, naturally.

808
01:01:25,727 --> 01:01:27,771
Which one of these has my suit in it?

809
01:01:27,854 --> 01:01:30,231
The small one underneath your right arm.

810
01:01:30,315 --> 01:01:32,734
Thanks. That ought to do the suit
a lot of good.

811
01:01:32,859 --> 01:01:35,195
I'm sure Mr. Kaplan won't mind
a few wrinkles.

812
01:01:35,403 --> 01:01:37,989
Yeah, if he's still there. What time is it?

813
01:01:38,406 --> 01:01:39,491
9:10.

814
01:01:39,574 --> 01:01:42,577
9:10! He may have left
his hotel room by now.

815
01:01:42,660 --> 01:01:45,497
I'll call him for you as soon
as we get inside the station.

816
01:01:45,538 --> 01:01:46,998
Thank you. I can do it.

817
01:01:47,040 --> 01:01:50,043
A redcap in a phone booth?
Slightly suspicious.

818
01:01:51,461 --> 01:01:54,214
All right. Do you know
what you'll tell him?

819
01:01:54,547 --> 01:01:58,176
You want to see him right away.
Terribly urgent. Matter of life and death.

820
01:01:58,259 --> 01:02:00,011
-No explanations.
-Right.

821
01:02:00,553 --> 01:02:03,014
While I'm calling,
you can change your clothes.

822
01:02:03,098 --> 01:02:06,559
Where do you propose I do that?
In Marshall Field's window?

823
01:02:06,643 --> 01:02:08,937
I sort of had the men's room in mind.

824
01:02:09,229 --> 01:02:10,522
Did you, now?

825
01:02:11,523 --> 01:02:15,026
You're the smartest girl
I ever spent the night with on a train.

826
01:02:26,705 --> 01:02:28,164
I think we made it.

827
01:02:32,043 --> 01:02:33,211
Where did he go?

828
01:02:33,294 --> 01:02:35,505
-Which way did he go?
-Where did he go?

829
01:02:35,588 --> 01:02:38,633
I don't know. He took my clothes.
He went up that way.

830
01:04:54,352 --> 01:04:55,645
What took you so long?

831
01:04:55,729 --> 01:04:59,065
I have a big face. Small razor.
Tell me, did you get Kaplan?

832
01:04:59,149 --> 01:05:00,066
Yes.

833
01:05:00,275 --> 01:05:01,693
Fine. What did he say?

834
01:05:01,735 --> 01:05:04,821
He'll see you but not at the hotel
under any circumstances.

835
01:05:04,904 --> 01:05:07,240
-He'll see you on the outside.
-Where? When?

836
01:05:07,323 --> 01:05:09,826
-I've got it all written down for you.
-Thanks.

837
01:05:09,909 --> 01:05:12,370
Take the Greyhound Bus
that leaves Chicago...

838
01:05:12,412 --> 01:05:17,417
...for Indianapolis at 2:00 and ask the driver
to let you off at Prairie Stop, Highway 41 .

839
01:05:18,501 --> 01:05:20,503
Prairie Stop, Highway 41 . Good.

840
01:05:20,587 --> 01:05:23,048
It's about an hour-and-a-half's drive
from Chicago.

841
01:05:23,089 --> 01:05:24,424
Fine. I'll rent a car.

842
01:05:24,507 --> 01:05:28,595
No car. Mr. Kaplan said bus.
He wants to be sure you're alone.

843
01:05:29,095 --> 01:05:31,723
All right. What'll I do when I get there?

844
01:05:31,806 --> 01:05:34,267
Just wait beside the road.
He'll be there at 3:30.

845
01:05:34,351 --> 01:05:36,394
-How will I know him?
-He'll know you.

846
01:05:36,478 --> 01:05:38,563
You made the Chicago papers, too.

847
01:05:40,440 --> 01:05:42,567
Have you set your watch for Central time?

848
01:05:42,734 --> 01:05:44,569
Yes, I did that. Thanks.

849
01:05:45,445 --> 01:05:46,946
What's the matter?

850
01:05:46,988 --> 01:05:47,781
Matter?

851
01:05:48,281 --> 01:05:50,700
Yes, you seem.... I don't know.
You seem tense.

852
01:05:50,784 --> 01:05:53,286
You better go before the police
run out of red caps.

853
01:05:53,828 --> 01:05:55,789
We'll see each other again, won't we?

854
01:05:56,623 --> 01:05:58,333
Sometime, I'm sure.

855
01:05:59,459 --> 01:06:01,961
I never had a moment
to thank you properly.

856
01:06:02,003 --> 01:06:04,339
-Please go.
-But where will I find you?

857
01:06:05,340 --> 01:06:07,050
I've got to pick up my bags now.

858
01:06:07,634 --> 01:06:10,553
Yes. These are the checks
for the large cases.

859
01:06:10,637 --> 01:06:11,888
Wait a minute.

860
01:06:12,597 --> 01:06:13,640
Please.

861
01:06:15,475 --> 01:06:16,476
They're coming.

862
01:10:44,160 --> 01:10:46,037
-Hot day.
-I've seen worse.

863
01:10:49,290 --> 01:10:51,584
Are you supposed
to be meeting someone here?

864
01:10:51,668 --> 01:10:54,379
I'm waiting for the bus.
It's due any minute.

865
01:10:55,922 --> 01:10:59,300
Some of them crop-duster pilots get rich
if they live long enough.

866
01:11:03,763 --> 01:11:05,724
Then your name isn't Kaplan?

867
01:11:06,433 --> 01:11:08,476
Can't say it is, 'cause it ain't.

868
01:11:09,436 --> 01:11:11,521
Here she comes. Right on time.

869
01:11:15,316 --> 01:11:16,901
-That's funny.
-What?

870
01:11:17,569 --> 01:11:20,196
That plane's dusting crops
where there ain't no crops.

871
01:15:13,888 --> 01:15:16,391
Get out of here! The other tank may blow!

872
01:15:27,694 --> 01:15:29,029
What happened?

873
01:15:55,430 --> 01:15:56,514
Come back!

874
01:15:57,307 --> 01:15:59,184
Come back! Come back!

875
01:16:51,277 --> 01:16:54,572
Could you let me have
Mr. George Kaplan's room number, please?

876
01:16:54,656 --> 01:16:55,824
Kaplan?

877
01:16:56,116 --> 01:16:57,909
I think he checked out.

878
01:16:59,119 --> 01:17:00,161
Checked out?

879
01:17:00,245 --> 01:17:03,081
That's right.
He checked out at 7:10 this morning.

880
01:17:03,164 --> 01:17:04,666
7:10? Are you sure?

881
01:17:04,874 --> 01:17:06,918
Yes. He left a forwarding address.

882
01:17:06,960 --> 01:17:10,005
Hotel Sheraton-Johnson,
Rapid City, South Dakota.

883
01:17:10,213 --> 01:17:11,297
7:10?

884
01:17:12,215 --> 01:17:14,801
Then how come I got a message
from him at 9:00....

885
01:17:14,884 --> 01:17:16,011
What's that?

886
01:17:19,556 --> 01:17:20,765
Nothing.

887
01:17:48,835 --> 01:17:50,879
Sorry to bother you again.

888
01:17:51,046 --> 01:17:54,132
Miss Eve Kendall is expecting me.
She's in room four-something.

889
01:17:54,174 --> 01:17:56,676
I've forgotten the number.
Would you mind?

890
01:18:06,144 --> 01:18:08,646
-She's in 463.
-Yes, that's right. Thanks.

891
01:18:35,215 --> 01:18:36,257
Hello.

892
01:18:42,764 --> 01:18:43,765
Surprised?

893
01:18:44,891 --> 01:18:45,850
Yes.

894
01:18:47,060 --> 01:18:48,895
No getting rid of me, is there?

895
01:19:00,824 --> 01:19:02,575
I could use a drink.

896
01:19:04,369 --> 01:19:05,745
I have some scotch.

897
01:19:05,787 --> 01:19:07,539
With water. No ice.

898
01:19:12,419 --> 01:19:16,047
Two Die As Crop-Duster Plane Crashes
Craft Hits Oil Tanker, Drivers Escape

899
01:19:16,423 --> 01:19:18,216
How did it go today?

900
01:19:21,511 --> 01:19:23,471
The meeting with Kaplan?

901
01:19:25,557 --> 01:19:27,225
He didn't show up.

902
01:19:29,769 --> 01:19:31,312
It's funny, isn't it?

903
01:19:32,272 --> 01:19:33,106
Why funny?

904
01:19:33,773 --> 01:19:38,069
After all those explicit directions
he gave you on the phone.

905
01:19:39,654 --> 01:19:41,406
Maybe I copied them down wrong.

906
01:19:41,990 --> 01:19:45,952
I don't think you got them wrong.
I think you sent me to the right place.

907
01:19:48,997 --> 01:19:52,042
Why don't you call him back again
and see what happened?

908
01:19:52,125 --> 01:19:55,170
I did. He checked out.
Went to South Dakota.

909
01:19:56,338 --> 01:19:57,589
South Dakota?

910
01:19:58,173 --> 01:19:59,466
Rapid City.

911
01:20:01,468 --> 01:20:03,136
What are you going to do next?

912
01:20:04,137 --> 01:20:05,722
I haven't made my mind up yet.

913
01:20:06,139 --> 01:20:07,932
It may depend on you.

914
01:20:09,559 --> 01:20:10,602
On me?

915
01:20:10,935 --> 01:20:12,020
Sure.

916
01:20:13,188 --> 01:20:15,398
You're my little helper, aren't you?

917
01:20:15,690 --> 01:20:16,775
Thank you.

918
01:20:16,858 --> 01:20:20,278
To us. To a long and lasting friendship.

919
01:20:20,945 --> 01:20:25,367
Meaning, from now on, I'm not going
to let you out of my sight, sweetheart.

920
01:20:25,825 --> 01:20:28,620
-I'm afraid you'll have to.
-Oh, no.

921
01:20:28,912 --> 01:20:32,957
I do have plans of my own, you know.
And you do have problems.

922
01:20:33,708 --> 01:20:38,380
Wouldn't it be nice if my problems
and your plans were somehow connected?

923
01:20:38,797 --> 01:20:41,257
Then we could always stay close
to each other...

924
01:20:41,341 --> 01:20:45,720
...and not have to go in separate directions.
Togetherness. You know what I mean?

925
01:20:49,849 --> 01:20:51,726
Go ahead, it can't be for me.

926
01:21:03,446 --> 01:21:05,240
No, not yet. I'm not ready.

927
01:21:06,241 --> 01:21:07,367
What time?

928
01:21:08,034 --> 01:21:10,328
I'll meet you. What's the address?

929
01:21:13,790 --> 01:21:15,208
Yes. Goodbye.

930
01:21:18,545 --> 01:21:19,713
Business?

931
01:21:20,213 --> 01:21:20,922
Yes.

932
01:21:21,006 --> 01:21:23,299
Industrial designing business?

933
01:21:26,886 --> 01:21:28,680
All work and no play.

934
01:21:29,305 --> 01:21:34,060
You should be enjoying yourself tonight
instead of taking phone calls from clients.

935
01:21:36,062 --> 01:21:38,356
What about having dinner with me?

936
01:21:38,523 --> 01:21:40,775
You can't afford to be seen anyplace.

937
01:21:40,859 --> 01:21:43,319
Let's have it up here. Nice and cozy.

938
01:21:43,403 --> 01:21:44,529
No, l....

939
01:21:45,905 --> 01:21:46,865
I can't.

940
01:21:48,533 --> 01:21:49,701
I insist.

941
01:21:51,745 --> 01:21:54,164
I want you to do a favor for me.

942
01:21:54,706 --> 01:21:56,791
-A big, big favor.
-Name it.

943
01:21:58,084 --> 01:21:59,878
I want you to leave right now.

944
01:22:00,670 --> 01:22:03,214
Stay far away from me
and don't come near me again.

945
01:22:06,760 --> 01:22:08,428
We're not going to get involved.

946
01:22:09,095 --> 01:22:11,681
Last night was last night
and that's all there was.

947
01:22:12,390 --> 01:22:15,602
That's all there is. There won't be
anything more between us.

948
01:22:16,269 --> 01:22:17,520
So, please....

949
01:22:18,229 --> 01:22:19,189
Goodbye.

950
01:22:19,731 --> 01:22:22,317
Good luck. No conversation. Just leave.

951
01:22:22,901 --> 01:22:23,860
Right away?

952
01:22:24,486 --> 01:22:25,320
Yes.

953
01:22:26,029 --> 01:22:27,405
No questions asked?

954
01:22:27,989 --> 01:22:28,823
Yes.

955
01:22:30,575 --> 01:22:31,743
I can't do that.

956
01:22:31,951 --> 01:22:32,952
Please.

957
01:22:33,495 --> 01:22:34,454
After dinner.

958
01:22:34,621 --> 01:22:35,580
Now.

959
01:22:35,872 --> 01:22:37,374
After dinner. Fair is fair.

960
01:22:40,085 --> 01:22:41,211
All right.

961
01:22:41,961 --> 01:22:43,338
On one condition.

962
01:22:44,547 --> 01:22:48,301
That you let the hotel valet
do something with this suit first.

963
01:22:48,385 --> 01:22:50,804
You belong in the stockyards
looking like that.

964
01:22:50,887 --> 01:22:51,846
Okay.

965
01:22:53,640 --> 01:22:55,100
There's the phone.

966
01:23:00,647 --> 01:23:02,357
Valet service, please.

967
01:23:10,115 --> 01:23:12,200
-Where are we?
-463.

968
01:23:12,826 --> 01:23:14,160
Room 463.

969
01:23:14,828 --> 01:23:17,414
How quickly can you get a suit
sponged and pressed?

970
01:23:17,497 --> 01:23:18,581
Yes, fast.

971
01:23:19,332 --> 01:23:21,292
20 minutes? Fine.

972
01:23:21,668 --> 01:23:23,044
Okay. 463.

973
01:23:26,464 --> 01:23:27,924
He'll be right up.

974
01:23:28,049 --> 01:23:30,260
Better take your things off.

975
01:23:33,221 --> 01:23:36,599
Now, what could a man do
with his clothes off for 20 minutes?

976
01:23:36,683 --> 01:23:38,309
Couldn't he have taken an hour?

977
01:23:38,393 --> 01:23:40,603
You could always take a cold shower.

978
01:23:40,687 --> 01:23:41,896
That's right.

979
01:23:43,148 --> 01:23:46,693
When I was a little boy,
I wouldn't even let my mother undress me.

980
01:23:46,776 --> 01:23:48,111
You're a big boy now.

981
01:23:52,741 --> 01:23:56,578
Tell me, how does a girl like you
get to be a girl like you?

982
01:23:58,038 --> 01:23:59,205
Lucky, I guess.

983
01:23:59,706 --> 01:24:02,667
Not lucky. Naughty. Wicked.
Up to no good.

984
01:24:03,084 --> 01:24:04,169
Ever kill anyone?

985
01:24:05,795 --> 01:24:09,215
Because I bet you could tease a man
to death without half trying.

986
01:24:09,299 --> 01:24:10,759
So, stop trying.

987
01:24:21,519 --> 01:24:23,188
Be with you in a minute.

988
01:24:28,526 --> 01:24:29,736
Trousers, please.

989
01:24:37,452 --> 01:24:38,703
Thank you.

990
01:24:40,080 --> 01:24:42,582
Think I'll take that cold shower after all.

991
01:24:42,707 --> 01:24:43,833
Good.

992
01:25:35,301 --> 01:25:36,094
1212 N. MICHIGAN

993
01:25:54,863 --> 01:25:57,532
This magnificent pair
of Louis Seize Fauteuils.

994
01:25:57,615 --> 01:26:01,202
Original gilt finish.
Upholstered in pure silk damask.

995
01:26:01,327 --> 01:26:04,456
How much may I say to start?
What am I bid?

996
01:26:04,497 --> 01:26:07,125
-$100.
-$100 is bid. Thank you.

997
01:26:07,542 --> 01:26:09,669
$150 is bid here. Say to $200?

998
01:26:10,086 --> 01:26:11,504
Thank you. $200 is bid.

999
01:26:11,546 --> 01:26:13,923
Say to $300? I have $300.

1000
01:26:14,632 --> 01:26:16,551
$400 by the little lady.

1001
01:26:16,760 --> 01:26:17,844
Thank you, sir.

1002
01:26:17,927 --> 01:26:20,472
$450 is bid for the pair. Can I hear $500?

1003
01:26:20,555 --> 01:26:21,973
Will you say $500?

1004
01:26:22,182 --> 01:26:23,850
Can I say the $500?

1005
01:26:24,392 --> 01:26:26,227
Fair warning and last call.

1006
01:26:26,394 --> 01:26:29,856
Sold to Mr. Stone, second row. $450.

1007
01:26:35,111 --> 01:26:36,654
Lot number 103.

1008
01:26:38,156 --> 01:26:39,991
This lovely Aubusson settee.

1009
01:26:40,533 --> 01:26:43,703
In excellent condition.
Please start the bidding.

1010
01:26:44,537 --> 01:26:45,538
How much?

1011
01:26:46,373 --> 01:26:49,959
$800 is offered, thank you.
$800 is bid. Say the nine? Go $900.

1012
01:26:50,293 --> 01:26:51,211
$900 is bid.

1013
01:26:51,294 --> 01:26:53,004
Now who'll say $1,000?

1014
01:26:53,463 --> 01:26:54,547
$1,000, thank you.

1015
01:26:54,631 --> 01:26:56,633
$1,000 at $1,000. Say $1,100.

1016
01:26:56,966 --> 01:26:59,386
Can I hear $1,100? Selling at $1,000.

1017
01:26:59,552 --> 01:27:01,346
The three of you together.

1018
01:27:01,638 --> 01:27:04,557
Now, that's a picture
only Charles Addams could draw.

1019
01:27:04,641 --> 01:27:06,184
Good evening, Mr. Kaplan.

1020
01:27:06,267 --> 01:27:09,604
Before we start calling each other names,
you'd better tell me yours.

1021
01:27:09,688 --> 01:27:12,357
-I haven't had the pleasure.
-You disappoint me, sir.

1022
01:27:13,274 --> 01:27:15,235
I was just going to say that to her.

1023
01:27:15,276 --> 01:27:18,279
I've always understood you were
a shrewd fellow at your job.

1024
01:27:18,363 --> 01:27:21,241
What possessed you to come
blundering in here like this?

1025
01:27:21,282 --> 01:27:23,451
Could it be an overpowering
interest in art?

1026
01:27:23,952 --> 01:27:25,704
Yes. The art of survival.

1027
01:27:27,831 --> 01:27:28,873
Have you poured any good drunks lately?

1028
01:27:31,251 --> 01:27:33,086
He followed me here from the hotel.

1029
01:27:34,129 --> 01:27:35,630
He was in your room?

1030
01:27:36,047 --> 01:27:37,382
Sure, isn't everybody?

1031
01:27:38,091 --> 01:27:40,427
$150. Thank you. Now, say the two.

1032
01:27:40,593 --> 01:27:41,678
Do I hear two?

1033
01:27:41,761 --> 01:27:42,971
$200, thank you.

1034
01:27:43,054 --> 01:27:45,890
Now the three. Do I hear three?
$300, anyone?

1035
01:27:45,932 --> 01:27:47,058
$300. Thank you.

1036
01:27:47,100 --> 01:27:49,519
Now, the four. Do I hear four?
$400, anyone?

1037
01:27:50,270 --> 01:27:51,271
$400 is bid.

1038
01:27:51,354 --> 01:27:53,440
Say the five. I have $400.

1039
01:27:54,315 --> 01:27:58,153
I didn't realize you were an art collector.
I thought you just collected corpses.

1040
01:27:59,487 --> 01:28:00,447
$500.

1041
01:28:00,947 --> 01:28:02,240
$500. Thank you.

1042
01:28:02,282 --> 01:28:05,577
I'll bet you paid plenty
for this little piece of sculpture.

1043
01:28:07,120 --> 01:28:08,079
$700.

1044
01:28:08,204 --> 01:28:10,623
She's worth every dollar of it,
take it from me.

1045
01:28:11,750 --> 01:28:13,918
She puts her heart into her work.

1046
01:28:14,002 --> 01:28:15,795
In fact her whole body.

1047
01:28:16,588 --> 01:28:18,548
Sold to Mr. Vandamm at $700.

1048
01:28:21,176 --> 01:28:23,219
Oh, Mr. Vandamm.

1049
01:28:24,512 --> 01:28:29,517
Has anyone told you that you overplay
your various roles rather severely?

1050
01:28:29,934 --> 01:28:32,437
First, you're the outraged
Madison Avenue man...

1051
01:28:32,479 --> 01:28:34,981
...who claims he's been mistaken
for someone else.

1052
01:28:35,023 --> 01:28:36,649
Then, you play the fugitive...

1053
01:28:36,733 --> 01:28:39,444
...trying to clear his name of a crime
he didn't commit.

1054
01:28:40,362 --> 01:28:42,781
Now you play the peevish lover...

1055
01:28:42,864 --> 01:28:44,783
...stung by jealousy and betrayal.

1056
01:28:45,909 --> 01:28:48,912
It seems you fellows could stand
less training from the FBI...

1057
01:28:48,995 --> 01:28:50,914
...and more from the Actors' Studio.

1058
01:28:52,082 --> 01:28:55,794
Apparently, the only performance
that will satisfy you is when I play dead.

1059
01:28:56,252 --> 01:28:59,673
Your very next role.
You'll be quite convincing, I assure you.

1060
01:29:00,924 --> 01:29:02,884
Thank you. $375 is bid.

1061
01:29:02,967 --> 01:29:04,803
I have $375, go to $400.

1062
01:29:04,844 --> 01:29:06,137
$375, go four?

1063
01:29:06,763 --> 01:29:07,681
$400 is bid.

1064
01:29:07,764 --> 01:29:11,726
I wonder what subtle form of manslaughter
is next on the program.

1065
01:29:12,185 --> 01:29:14,771
Am I to be dropped
into a vat of molten steel...

1066
01:29:14,854 --> 01:29:17,190
...and become part of a new skyscraper?

1067
01:29:17,691 --> 01:29:21,945
Or are you going to ask this female
to kiss me again and poison me to death?

1068
01:29:25,073 --> 01:29:28,451
Who are you kidding?
You have no feelings to hurt.

1069
01:29:36,876 --> 01:29:38,837
We've had just about enough of you.

1070
01:29:38,920 --> 01:29:41,006
Then, why don't you send for the police?

1071
01:29:43,049 --> 01:29:46,553
That's the last thing you'd want, isn't it?
Me in the hands of the police.

1072
01:29:46,636 --> 01:29:48,513
There's something I might tell them.

1073
01:29:48,555 --> 01:29:51,349
That's why you had her hustle me
on the train last night.

1074
01:29:51,683 --> 01:29:55,478
Something tells me I've got a better
chance of survival if I go to the police.

1075
01:29:57,063 --> 01:29:59,899
Good night, sweetheart.
Don't think it wasn't nice.

1076
01:30:00,692 --> 01:30:04,154
$1,200 there. $1,200 is bid. Say $1,300?

1077
01:30:04,404 --> 01:30:06,197
May I hear $1,300?

1078
01:30:06,656 --> 01:30:09,200
They're selling at $1,200.
Do I hear $1,300, please?

1079
01:30:10,410 --> 01:30:11,494
Last call.

1080
01:30:12,203 --> 01:30:15,081
Sold. $1,200. Thank you.

1081
01:30:22,297 --> 01:30:23,882
Catalogue number 109.

1082
01:30:25,091 --> 01:30:28,470
This superb example
of this early 17th century master.

1083
01:30:29,054 --> 01:30:31,389
It will enhance any collection of fine art.

1084
01:30:32,140 --> 01:30:34,517
What is your pleasure?
How much to start?

1085
01:30:35,477 --> 01:30:36,936
$1,000.

1086
01:30:37,270 --> 01:30:38,605
$1,000 is bid.

1087
01:30:39,272 --> 01:30:41,274
$1,250 I have. Now $1,500.

1088
01:30:43,026 --> 01:30:44,736
$1,500 is bid. Thank you.

1089
01:30:44,819 --> 01:30:45,904
$1,750?

1090
01:30:47,781 --> 01:30:49,157
I have $1,750.

1091
01:30:49,282 --> 01:30:50,241
$2,000.

1092
01:30:50,325 --> 01:30:53,745
$2,000 is bid. I have $2,000.
Do I hear $2,500?

1093
01:30:54,996 --> 01:30:56,331
$2,500, anyone?

1094
01:30:57,290 --> 01:30:58,375
$2,250 once.

1095
01:31:00,001 --> 01:31:01,294
$2,250 twice.

1096
01:31:01,961 --> 01:31:03,088
Last call.

1097
01:31:03,296 --> 01:31:04,297
$1,500.

1098
01:31:05,882 --> 01:31:08,259
But the bid is already up to $2,250, sir.

1099
01:31:08,301 --> 01:31:09,803
I still say $1,500.

1100
01:31:12,430 --> 01:31:14,099
I have $2,250.

1101
01:31:14,641 --> 01:31:16,101
Do I hear $2,500?

1102
01:31:16,726 --> 01:31:18,937
$2,250 once, $2,250 twice.

1103
01:31:19,270 --> 01:31:20,271
$1,200.

1104
01:31:20,480 --> 01:31:22,524
Sold for $2,250. And now--

1105
01:31:22,691 --> 01:31:24,651
$2,250, for that chromo?

1106
01:31:29,614 --> 01:31:31,783
Number 1 10 in the catalogue.

1107
01:31:32,409 --> 01:31:35,120
A Louis Quinze carved
and gilded lit de repos.

1108
01:31:35,662 --> 01:31:38,456
Would somebody start the bidding
at $750, please?

1109
01:31:38,790 --> 01:31:41,751
How do we know it's not a fake?
It looks like a fake.

1110
01:31:44,921 --> 01:31:48,967
One thing we know. You're no fake.
You're a genuine idiot.

1111
01:31:49,467 --> 01:31:50,343
Thank you.

1112
01:31:51,177 --> 01:31:52,637
I wonder if I could...

1113
01:31:52,721 --> 01:31:55,974
...ask the gentleman to get into the spirit
of the proceedings.

1114
01:31:56,016 --> 01:31:57,809
All right. I'll start it at $800.

1115
01:31:58,768 --> 01:32:00,145
$800. Thank you.

1116
01:32:00,895 --> 01:32:01,563
$900?

1117
01:32:02,272 --> 01:32:04,232
$1,000 is bid. Go $1,200.

1118
01:32:04,691 --> 01:32:05,483
$1,100.

1119
01:32:05,859 --> 01:32:08,194
$1,100 is bid. Thank you. Go $1,200.

1120
01:32:08,987 --> 01:32:10,113
I have $1,100.

1121
01:32:10,196 --> 01:32:11,990
Go $1,200. Who'll say $1,200?

1122
01:32:12,657 --> 01:32:14,659
$1,100 once. Who'll say $1,200?

1123
01:32:15,035 --> 01:32:16,036
$1,100 twice.

1124
01:32:16,119 --> 01:32:17,537
$1,200. Thank you.

1125
01:32:17,620 --> 01:32:19,789
$1,200 is bid. I have $1,200. Go $1,300.

1126
01:32:19,873 --> 01:32:21,166
Who'll say $1,300?

1127
01:32:21,207 --> 01:32:22,208
$13.

1128
01:32:25,045 --> 01:32:26,296
You mean $1,300, sir.

1129
01:32:27,881 --> 01:32:30,258
No, I mean $13.
That's more than it's worth.

1130
01:32:34,429 --> 01:32:35,722
I have $1,200.

1131
01:32:35,930 --> 01:32:37,891
Go $1,300. Who'll say $1,300?

1132
01:32:38,975 --> 01:32:40,560
Who'll say $1,250?

1133
01:32:43,229 --> 01:32:44,481
$1,200 once.

1134
01:32:44,564 --> 01:32:45,607
$1,200 twice.

1135
01:32:46,232 --> 01:32:47,817
Last call. $1,200.

1136
01:32:47,901 --> 01:32:48,902
$2,000.

1137
01:32:52,155 --> 01:32:53,114
$2,000?

1138
01:32:55,408 --> 01:32:56,201
$2,100.

1139
01:32:57,577 --> 01:32:59,162
I'm sorry, but we can't--

1140
01:32:59,245 --> 01:33:00,663
Make it $2,500.

1141
01:33:06,336 --> 01:33:08,672
Would the gentleman please cooperate?

1142
01:33:08,755 --> 01:33:10,215
The last bid was $1,200.

1143
01:33:10,799 --> 01:33:12,926
$2,500. My money is as good
as anybody's.

1144
01:33:15,345 --> 01:33:16,388
I have--

1145
01:33:17,055 --> 01:33:18,765
-What was it?
-$1,200.

1146
01:33:19,683 --> 01:33:22,560
I have $1,200 once, $1,200 twice--

1147
01:33:22,936 --> 01:33:23,937
$3,000.

1148
01:33:23,978 --> 01:33:25,063
Sold for $1,200.

1149
01:33:28,692 --> 01:33:31,945
-I won't let you get away with that.
-You'd better leave, sir.

1150
01:33:32,028 --> 01:33:34,155
Take your hands off me or I'll sue you.

1151
01:33:49,045 --> 01:33:51,506
-What took you so long?
-Let's take a little walk.

1152
01:33:51,589 --> 01:33:52,465
Get moving.

1153
01:33:52,549 --> 01:33:55,468
I haven't finished bidding yet.
$3,000. I bid $3,000.

1154
01:33:56,428 --> 01:33:58,930
I'm sorry, old man. Too bad. Keep trying.

1155
01:34:00,515 --> 01:34:01,891
Not so rough!

1156
01:34:10,442 --> 01:34:12,610
-Handle with care, fellas.
-In there.

1157
01:34:12,652 --> 01:34:14,446
-I'm valuable property.
-In.

1158
01:34:18,825 --> 01:34:22,787
I want to thank you for saving my life.
Thank you, my friend. Thank you.

1159
01:34:22,912 --> 01:34:24,831
Save it for the stationhouse.

1160
01:34:29,544 --> 01:34:32,130
Let's have some smiles and good cheer.

1161
01:34:32,339 --> 01:34:35,091
You're about to become heroes.
Don't you know who I am?

1162
01:34:36,468 --> 01:34:40,221
We'll find out as soon as we book you
for being drunk and disorderly.

1163
01:34:40,597 --> 01:34:43,767
That's chicken feed. You've hit the jackpot.

1164
01:34:44,017 --> 01:34:47,395
"Chicago police capture
United Nations killer."

1165
01:34:48,772 --> 01:34:50,565
My name is Roger Thornhill.

1166
01:34:51,107 --> 01:34:52,067
Here.

1167
01:34:55,820 --> 01:34:58,531
-It's him.
-That's right. Congratulations.

1168
01:35:00,867 --> 01:35:03,078
This is 1055. Sergeant Flamm.

1169
01:35:03,828 --> 01:35:07,374
We've got a man here who answers
to the description of Thornhill, Roger.

1170
01:35:07,916 --> 01:35:09,334
Code 76.

1171
01:35:09,709 --> 01:35:12,629
Wanted by NYPD. Positive ID.

1172
01:35:14,255 --> 01:35:15,840
Absolutely. No question.

1173
01:35:17,634 --> 01:35:20,720
Michigan Avenue. Proceeding north
to 42nd precinct.

1174
01:35:24,140 --> 01:35:25,141
What?

1175
01:35:27,185 --> 01:35:28,061
Come again!

1176
01:35:30,647 --> 01:35:31,856
Are you sure?

1177
01:35:33,233 --> 01:35:34,734
Okay. Right.

1178
01:35:35,235 --> 01:35:36,528
Yeah, I got it.

1179
01:35:36,820 --> 01:35:38,488
1055, off and clear.

1180
01:35:41,157 --> 01:35:43,243
-Where are we going?
-The airport.

1181
01:35:44,077 --> 01:35:45,704
-For what?
-Orders.

1182
01:35:46,538 --> 01:35:50,834
Airport? I'm not going to any airport.
I want to be taken to police headquarters.

1183
01:35:51,084 --> 01:35:52,210
You do, huh?

1184
01:35:52,877 --> 01:35:54,671
Why do you think I sent for you?

1185
01:35:54,754 --> 01:35:57,340
How about this guy? He sent for us!

1186
01:35:57,674 --> 01:35:58,591
Sit back!

1187
01:35:58,633 --> 01:36:01,511
Didn't you hear? I want to be taken
to police headquarters.

1188
01:36:01,594 --> 01:36:04,597
I'm a dangerous assassin.
I'm a mad killer on the loose!

1189
01:36:04,681 --> 01:36:06,891
You ought to be ashamed of yourself.

1190
01:36:18,361 --> 01:36:23,033
NORTHWEST

1191
01:36:33,335 --> 01:36:34,627
They said right here.

1192
01:36:34,711 --> 01:36:37,922
Does anyone mind if I sit down?
I've been running all day.

1193
01:36:49,184 --> 01:36:52,312
Thought I'd never make it.
Getting too old for this kind of work.

1194
01:36:52,896 --> 01:36:54,397
All right, men. Thank you.

1195
01:36:54,981 --> 01:36:57,901
This way, Mr. Thornhill.
We haven't got much time.

1196
01:36:58,318 --> 01:37:00,111
This way is more private.

1197
01:37:04,032 --> 01:37:05,784
I don't think I caught your name.

1198
01:37:05,992 --> 01:37:07,786
I don't think I pitched it.

1199
01:37:08,536 --> 01:37:10,914
You're police, aren't you? Or is it FBI?

1200
01:37:11,331 --> 01:37:15,043
FBI, CIA, ONI.
We're all in the same alphabet soup.

1201
01:37:15,794 --> 01:37:19,964
You can stick this in your alphabet soup.
I had nothing to do with that killing.

1202
01:37:20,006 --> 01:37:21,966
-We know that.
-You know it?

1203
01:37:22,092 --> 01:37:24,928
Then why did you let the police
chase me all over the map?

1204
01:37:25,011 --> 01:37:28,765
We never interfere with the police
unless absolutely necessary.

1205
01:37:29,349 --> 01:37:31,017
It has become necessary.

1206
01:37:31,851 --> 01:37:33,978
Then, I take it I'm to be cleared.

1207
01:37:34,145 --> 01:37:37,148
I wish you'd walk faster, Mr. Thornhill.
We'll miss the plane.

1208
01:37:37,190 --> 01:37:39,484
Where are we going?
New York or Washington?

1209
01:37:39,526 --> 01:37:41,569
Rapid City, South Dakota.

1210
01:37:41,945 --> 01:37:43,822
Rapid City? What for?

1211
01:37:44,030 --> 01:37:45,532
It's near Mt. Rushmore.

1212
01:37:45,615 --> 01:37:47,659
No, thank you. I've seen Mt. Rushmore.

1213
01:37:47,701 --> 01:37:49,703
So has your friend Mr. Vandamm.

1214
01:37:50,704 --> 01:37:51,830
Vandamm?

1215
01:37:52,539 --> 01:37:54,332
A rather formidable gentleman.

1216
01:37:55,417 --> 01:37:57,877
What about that treacherous
little tramp with him?

1217
01:37:57,961 --> 01:37:59,170
Miss Kendall?

1218
01:37:59,379 --> 01:38:01,631
His mistress. We know all about her.

1219
01:38:03,133 --> 01:38:05,260
Tell me, what's Vandamm up to?

1220
01:38:05,343 --> 01:38:08,179
You could say he's a sort
of importer-exporter.

1221
01:38:08,430 --> 01:38:09,472
Of what?

1222
01:38:11,266 --> 01:38:12,767
Government secrets, perhaps.

1223
01:38:13,351 --> 01:38:15,020
Then why don't you grab him?

1224
01:38:15,145 --> 01:38:18,064
There's still too much we don't know
about his organization.

1225
01:38:18,148 --> 01:38:21,818
I see. What's all this got to do
with Mt. Rushmore?

1226
01:38:22,444 --> 01:38:24,446
Vandamm has a place near there.

1227
01:38:24,571 --> 01:38:27,991
We think it's his jumping-off point
to leave the country tomorrow night.

1228
01:38:28,074 --> 01:38:30,493
-Are you going to stop him?
-No.

1229
01:38:30,577 --> 01:38:32,037
Then why are we going there?

1230
01:38:32,579 --> 01:38:34,706
To set his mind at ease
about George Kaplan.

1231
01:38:36,374 --> 01:38:37,542
Oh, you, huh?

1232
01:38:37,584 --> 01:38:39,461
You're George Kaplan, aren't you?

1233
01:38:39,544 --> 01:38:42,297
There is no such person as George Kaplan.

1234
01:38:42,380 --> 01:38:44,716
What do you mean? I've been in his room.

1235
01:38:44,758 --> 01:38:47,761
I've tried on his clothes.
He's got short sleeves and dandruff!

1236
01:38:47,802 --> 01:38:49,846
Believe me, he doesn't exist.

1237
01:38:50,764 --> 01:38:55,393
Which is why I'm going to have to ask you
to go on being him for the next 24 hours.

1238
01:38:56,853 --> 01:38:59,272
Come on! We'll discuss it on the plane.

1239
01:39:21,336 --> 01:39:24,923
You started the decoy business
without me, you finish it without me!

1240
01:39:25,006 --> 01:39:27,258
We might have
if you hadn't stumbled into it.

1241
01:39:27,300 --> 01:39:31,137
You should give me a medal and a vacation
instead of asking me to be a target...

1242
01:39:31,221 --> 01:39:34,015
...just so your special agent
doesn't get shot at!

1243
01:39:34,307 --> 01:39:36,643
Not shot at, Mr. Thornhill. Found out.

1244
01:39:36,935 --> 01:39:39,270
Once found out they're as good as dead.

1245
01:39:39,521 --> 01:39:42,816
Thanks to you,
clouds of suspicion are already forming.

1246
01:39:43,483 --> 01:39:44,776
Thanks to me?

1247
01:39:46,111 --> 01:39:47,612
You listen to me.

1248
01:39:47,821 --> 01:39:50,156
I'm an advertising man, not a red herring!

1249
01:39:50,240 --> 01:39:52,409
I've got a job, a secretary, a mother...

1250
01:39:52,492 --> 01:39:55,412
...two ex-wives and several bartenders
dependent upon me...

1251
01:39:55,495 --> 01:39:58,623
...and I don't intend to disappoint them
by getting myself killed!

1252
01:39:59,165 --> 01:40:00,542
The answer is no.

1253
01:40:01,459 --> 01:40:02,961
-Is that final?
-Yes.

1254
01:40:03,878 --> 01:40:05,547
-Goodbye, then.
-Goodbye.

1255
01:40:06,047 --> 01:40:09,592
If there was a chance of changing
your mind I'd talk about Miss Kendall...

1256
01:40:10,510 --> 01:40:12,512
...of whom you so obviously disapprove.

1257
01:40:12,595 --> 01:40:16,182
Yes, for using sex
like some people use a fly-swatter.

1258
01:40:16,683 --> 01:40:18,601
I suppose it doesn't matter to you...

1259
01:40:18,685 --> 01:40:22,731
...that she was probably forced to do
whatever she did to protect herself.

1260
01:40:23,023 --> 01:40:25,233
To protect herself from what?

1261
01:40:25,316 --> 01:40:27,485
Exposure, assassination.

1262
01:40:29,154 --> 01:40:31,364
You see, Mr. Thornhill, she's....

1263
01:40:32,032 --> 01:40:33,950
She's one of our agents.

1264
01:40:34,993 --> 01:40:35,952
Oh, no!

1265
01:40:36,036 --> 01:40:37,662
I know you didn't mean it...

1266
01:40:37,746 --> 01:40:41,708
...but I'm afraid you have put her
in an extremely dangerous situation.

1267
01:40:43,543 --> 01:40:45,962
And much more than her life is at stake.

1268
01:41:00,560 --> 01:41:01,978
Suppose they don't come?

1269
01:41:02,437 --> 01:41:03,855
They'll come.

1270
01:41:05,482 --> 01:41:08,693
I don't like the way Teddy Roosevelt
is looking at me.

1271
01:41:08,985 --> 01:41:12,906
Perhaps he's trying to give you
one last word of caution, Mr. Kaplan.

1272
01:41:12,947 --> 01:41:14,991
"Speak softly, and carry a big stick."

1273
01:41:15,450 --> 01:41:19,079
He's trying to tell me not to go through
with this harebrained scheme.

1274
01:41:19,162 --> 01:41:22,665
He doesn't know to what extent
you are the cause of our present trouble.

1275
01:41:22,749 --> 01:41:25,335
I don't know that I care
to accept that charge.

1276
01:41:25,627 --> 01:41:30,465
If you hadn't made yourself so attractive
to Miss Kendall that she fell for you...

1277
01:41:30,548 --> 01:41:31,716
And vice versa.

1278
01:41:31,758 --> 01:41:35,053
...our friend Vandamm wouldn't be
losing faith in her loyalty now.

1279
01:41:35,512 --> 01:41:39,474
It was quite obvious to him last night
that she had become emotionally involved.

1280
01:41:39,557 --> 01:41:43,228
Worst of all, with a man he thinks
is a government agent.

1281
01:41:44,187 --> 01:41:46,564
Are you trying to tell me
that I'm irresistible?

1282
01:41:46,606 --> 01:41:49,234
I'm trying to remind you
that it's your responsibility...

1283
01:41:49,317 --> 01:41:51,986
...to help us restore her
to Vandamm's good graces...

1284
01:41:52,070 --> 01:41:54,155
...until he leaves the country tonight.

1285
01:41:54,989 --> 01:41:56,282
All right, all right.

1286
01:41:56,866 --> 01:41:58,451
But, after tonight....

1287
01:41:59,452 --> 01:42:01,454
My blessings on you both!

1288
01:42:16,094 --> 01:42:17,053
Here they are!

1289
01:43:11,149 --> 01:43:12,609
Good afternoon, Mr. Kaplan.

1290
01:43:12,692 --> 01:43:13,693
Not her!

1291
01:43:23,286 --> 01:43:26,081
Did I misunderstand you
about bringing her here?

1292
01:43:26,164 --> 01:43:28,375
We'll get to that later.

1293
01:43:29,167 --> 01:43:31,378
I suppose you were surprised
to get my call.

1294
01:43:31,461 --> 01:43:35,215
Not at all. I knew the police
would release you, Mr. Kaplan.

1295
01:43:35,465 --> 01:43:38,510
By the way, I want to compliment you
on your colorful exit...

1296
01:43:38,593 --> 01:43:40,553
...from the auction gallery.

1297
01:43:40,929 --> 01:43:42,097
Thank you.

1298
01:43:42,180 --> 01:43:45,225
And now what little drama
are we here for today?

1299
01:43:45,308 --> 01:43:49,187
I don't for a moment believe that
you invited me to these gay surroundings...

1300
01:43:49,229 --> 01:43:51,523
...to come to a business arrangement.

1301
01:43:53,066 --> 01:43:57,404
Suppose I tell you that I not only know
when you're leaving the country tonight...

1302
01:43:57,487 --> 01:44:00,031
...but the latitude and longitude
of your rendezvous...

1303
01:44:00,115 --> 01:44:02,200
...and your ultimate destination.

1304
01:44:02,659 --> 01:44:05,286
You wouldn't care to carry my bags,
would you?

1305
01:44:05,537 --> 01:44:08,665
Perhaps you'd be interested in the price,
just the same.

1306
01:44:08,873 --> 01:44:09,958
The price?

1307
01:44:10,041 --> 01:44:12,043
For doing nothing to stop you.

1308
01:44:13,670 --> 01:44:15,964
How much did you have in mind?

1309
01:44:16,589 --> 01:44:17,716
I want the girl.

1310
01:44:20,677 --> 01:44:22,721
I want her to get what's coming to her.

1311
01:44:23,763 --> 01:44:27,350
Turn her over to me. I'll see there's
enough pinned on her to keep her...

1312
01:44:27,434 --> 01:44:29,561
...uncomfortable for the rest of her life.

1313
01:44:30,812 --> 01:44:33,690
You do that,
and I'll look the other way tonight.

1314
01:44:34,232 --> 01:44:36,192
She really did get under your skin.

1315
01:44:36,568 --> 01:44:39,446
We're not talking about my skin.
We're talking about yours.

1316
01:44:39,487 --> 01:44:42,824
-I'm offering you a chance to save it.
-To exchange it!

1317
01:44:44,117 --> 01:44:45,493
Put it any way you like.

1318
01:44:45,994 --> 01:44:48,038
I'm curious, Mr. Kaplan.

1319
01:44:48,580 --> 01:44:52,876
What made you think that my feelings
for her may have deteriorated...

1320
01:44:52,959 --> 01:44:56,296
...to the point where I would trade her in
for a little peace of mind?

1321
01:44:57,630 --> 01:44:59,090
I don't deduce.

1322
01:44:59,299 --> 01:45:00,425
I observe.

1323
01:45:03,136 --> 01:45:06,431
Phillip, if you don't mind,
I'm going back to the house now.

1324
01:45:18,902 --> 01:45:20,111
Just a second, you.

1325
01:45:20,153 --> 01:45:23,073
Stay away from me! Let go!
Let go of me!

1326
01:45:24,157 --> 01:45:26,159
Stay away from me! Let go!

1327
01:45:26,326 --> 01:45:27,577
Let go of me!

1328
01:45:28,161 --> 01:45:29,621
Save the phony tears.

1329
01:45:29,662 --> 01:45:31,164
You just get back.

1330
01:45:33,416 --> 01:45:34,501
You little fool!

1331
01:45:34,584 --> 01:45:36,378
You just stay away from me.

1332
01:45:42,008 --> 01:45:44,219
No good. You can't get involved in this.

1333
01:45:48,765 --> 01:45:50,892
-Don't touch anything.
-Stand back.

1334
01:45:51,226 --> 01:45:52,143
Stand back.

1335
01:46:53,413 --> 01:46:54,706
Don't be long.

1336
01:46:58,626 --> 01:46:59,669
Hello.

1337
01:47:01,004 --> 01:47:02,047
Hello.

1338
01:47:02,881 --> 01:47:04,257
Are you all right?

1339
01:47:05,508 --> 01:47:07,052
Yes, I think so.

1340
01:47:07,635 --> 01:47:10,347
I asked the Professor
if I could see you again.

1341
01:47:12,807 --> 01:47:14,684
There's not much time.

1342
01:47:16,144 --> 01:47:17,479
Isn't there?

1343
01:47:18,355 --> 01:47:20,273
I wanted to tell you....

1344
01:47:20,648 --> 01:47:22,108
I mean, apologize.

1345
01:47:23,610 --> 01:47:24,694
No need.

1346
01:47:26,112 --> 01:47:27,447
I understand.

1347
01:47:29,908 --> 01:47:31,576
All in the line of duty.

1348
01:47:33,161 --> 01:47:35,246
I did treat you miserably.

1349
01:47:37,457 --> 01:47:39,167
I hated you for it.

1350
01:47:39,918 --> 01:47:41,961
I didn't want you to go on thinking....

1351
01:47:44,798 --> 01:47:47,258
I used some pretty harsh words, I'm sorry.

1352
01:47:47,676 --> 01:47:50,387
-They hurt deeply.
-Naturally, if I'd known....

1353
01:47:50,804 --> 01:47:52,430
I couldn't tell you.

1354
01:47:53,306 --> 01:47:54,683
-Of course not.
-Could l?

1355
01:47:56,559 --> 01:47:57,936
No, I guess not.

1356
01:47:59,979 --> 01:48:02,273
You didn't get hurt. I'm so relieved.

1357
01:48:02,357 --> 01:48:04,317
Of course I was. How would you have felt?

1358
01:48:04,359 --> 01:48:07,821
I mean in the cafeteria, when you fell.
When I shot you with the blanks.

1359
01:48:07,904 --> 01:48:09,406
Oh, that. No.

1360
01:48:10,323 --> 01:48:12,283
You did it rather well, I thought.

1361
01:48:12,325 --> 01:48:14,619
Yes, I thought I was quite graceful.

1362
01:48:15,203 --> 01:48:17,789
Considering it's not really
your kind of work.

1363
01:48:19,165 --> 01:48:22,544
No, I got into it by accident.
What's your excuse?

1364
01:48:24,796 --> 01:48:28,633
I met Phillip Vandamm at a party one night
and saw only his charm.

1365
01:48:32,095 --> 01:48:35,015
I guess I had nothing to do that weekend...

1366
01:48:35,056 --> 01:48:37,350
...so I decided to fall in love.

1367
01:48:37,976 --> 01:48:39,310
That's nice.

1368
01:48:41,104 --> 01:48:45,108
Eventually, the Professor and his
Washington colleagues approached me...

1369
01:48:46,609 --> 01:48:50,321
...with a few sordid details about Phillip
and he told me that...

1370
01:48:51,322 --> 01:48:55,285
...my relationship with him
made me "uniquely valuable" to them.

1371
01:48:56,995 --> 01:48:58,997
So you became a Girl Scout?

1372
01:49:00,248 --> 01:49:03,543
It was the first time anyone ever asked me
to do anything worthwhile.

1373
01:49:05,920 --> 01:49:07,339
Has life been like that?

1374
01:49:10,592 --> 01:49:11,426
How come?

1375
01:49:14,220 --> 01:49:15,430
Men like you.

1376
01:49:17,098 --> 01:49:19,392
What's wrong with men like me?

1377
01:49:19,684 --> 01:49:21,978
They don't believe in marriage.

1378
01:49:22,062 --> 01:49:23,855
I've been married twice.

1379
01:49:23,897 --> 01:49:25,231
See what I mean?

1380
01:49:27,484 --> 01:49:30,195
I may go back to hating you.
It was more fun.

1381
01:49:37,035 --> 01:49:38,620
Goodbye, darling.

1382
01:49:39,996 --> 01:49:41,164
Wait. Not so soon.

1383
01:49:41,539 --> 01:49:43,375
I must get back and convince them...

1384
01:49:43,458 --> 01:49:46,461
...that I took the long way
so nobody would follow me.

1385
01:49:48,505 --> 01:49:50,882
Couldn't we stand like this
for just a few hours?

1386
01:49:50,924 --> 01:49:53,510
Just this time you're supposed
to be critically wounded!

1387
01:49:53,593 --> 01:49:55,261
I never felt more alive.

1388
01:49:55,637 --> 01:49:56,930
Whose side are you on?

1389
01:49:58,181 --> 01:49:59,516
Yours, always, darling.

1390
01:50:00,642 --> 01:50:04,187
Please don't undermine my resolve,
just when I need it most.

1391
01:50:12,779 --> 01:50:16,616
I guess it's off to the hospital for me
and back to danger for you.

1392
01:50:16,783 --> 01:50:18,535
I don't like it a bit.

1393
01:50:18,785 --> 01:50:22,288
It's much safer now, thanks to you,
my darling decoy.

1394
01:50:22,872 --> 01:50:25,083
Don't thank me. I couldn't stand it.

1395
01:50:25,125 --> 01:50:26,835
All right. I won't.

1396
01:50:26,918 --> 01:50:30,130
After your malevolent friend Vandamm
takes off tonight...

1397
01:50:30,296 --> 01:50:34,426
...we'll get together and do a lot of
apologizing to each other, in private.

1398
01:50:34,551 --> 01:50:36,094
You know that can't be.

1399
01:50:36,136 --> 01:50:37,929
Of course it can be!

1400
01:50:41,182 --> 01:50:43,268
He has told you, hasn't he?

1401
01:50:43,810 --> 01:50:45,020
Told me what?

1402
01:50:45,645 --> 01:50:47,105
You've got to get moving.

1403
01:50:48,064 --> 01:50:50,275
-What didn't you tell me?
-Why didn't you?

1404
01:50:51,317 --> 01:50:54,154
She's going off with Vandamm tonight
on the plane.

1405
01:50:56,197 --> 01:50:57,782
She's going off with Vandamm?

1406
01:50:57,824 --> 01:51:01,244
That's why we went to such lengths
to make her a fugitive from justice.

1407
01:51:01,327 --> 01:51:04,164
So that Vandamm couldn't decline
to take her along.

1408
01:51:04,330 --> 01:51:07,250
I needn't tell you how valuable
she can be to us over there.

1409
01:51:08,001 --> 01:51:10,253
You lied to me!
You said that after tonight--

1410
01:51:10,462 --> 01:51:11,671
I needed your help.

1411
01:51:11,755 --> 01:51:13,048
You got it, all right.

1412
01:51:13,131 --> 01:51:14,341
Don't be angry.

1413
01:51:14,424 --> 01:51:17,344
-You think I'll let you go through with this?
-She has to.

1414
01:51:17,677 --> 01:51:19,512
Nobody has to do anything!

1415
01:51:19,679 --> 01:51:21,681
I don't like the games you play!

1416
01:51:22,682 --> 01:51:25,560
War is hell, Mr. Thornhill,
even when it's a cold one.

1417
01:51:26,561 --> 01:51:28,897
If you can't lick the Vandamms
of this world...

1418
01:51:29,356 --> 01:51:32,275
...without asking girls to bed down
and fly away with them...

1419
01:51:32,484 --> 01:51:34,152
...and probably never come back...

1420
01:51:34,235 --> 01:51:36,988
...perhaps you should learn
how to lose a few cold wars.

1421
01:51:37,614 --> 01:51:39,074
We're already doing that.

1422
01:51:46,748 --> 01:51:50,460
-I won't let you do this. Get out.
-Please don't spoil everything.

1423
01:51:55,965 --> 01:52:00,220
...shot Mr. Kaplan twice in full view
of horrified men, women and children...

1424
01:52:00,303 --> 01:52:03,682
...who had come to the park
to see the famed Mt. Rushmore monument.

1425
01:52:03,723 --> 01:52:09,354
Witnesses described the assailant as
an attractive blonde in her late twenties.

1426
01:52:09,562 --> 01:52:13,400
Kaplan, who was taken to the hospital
in critical condition...

1427
01:52:13,483 --> 01:52:17,570
...has been tentatively identified
as an employee of the federal government.

1428
01:52:17,654 --> 01:52:20,240
The tragedy developed
with startling suddenness.

1429
01:52:20,323 --> 01:52:23,493
Chris Swenson,
a busboy in the Mt. Rushmore cafeteria...

1430
01:52:23,576 --> 01:52:26,538
...stated that he heard voices raised in--

1431
01:52:32,627 --> 01:52:33,795
Here we are.

1432
01:52:34,879 --> 01:52:37,215
Slacks, a shirt and these.

1433
01:52:37,757 --> 01:52:38,967
Thanks.

1434
01:52:39,050 --> 01:52:42,303
That'll do for you around here
for the next couple of days.

1435
01:52:43,304 --> 01:52:45,265
The next couple of days?

1436
01:52:46,474 --> 01:52:47,934
Hey, what's this?

1437
01:52:48,643 --> 01:52:51,896
That's where I hit the chair
doing that phony fall in the cafeteria.

1438
01:52:51,938 --> 01:52:54,357
You and your dopey schemes. Shooting.

1439
01:52:54,441 --> 01:52:57,402
-Otherwise, feeling all right?
-Oh, yes. Fine.

1440
01:52:58,278 --> 01:53:01,489
Considering your driver has
a sledgehammer for a hand.

1441
01:53:01,656 --> 01:53:03,491
Yeah, I'm sorry about that.

1442
01:53:03,658 --> 01:53:06,202
That's all right. I guess I deserved it.

1443
01:53:06,953 --> 01:53:09,289
I guess I deserve that locked door, too.

1444
01:53:09,372 --> 01:53:13,209
If you were seen wandering about healthy,
it could prove fatal to Miss Kendall.

1445
01:53:14,210 --> 01:53:16,921
-I've begun to forget her already.
-Good.

1446
01:53:17,464 --> 01:53:19,632
-Better that way.
-Yeah, much.

1447
01:53:20,091 --> 01:53:22,260
Inside of an hour, she'll be gone.

1448
01:53:25,305 --> 01:53:27,182
How's everything out in Rapid City?

1449
01:53:27,265 --> 01:53:28,933
Everything's fine.

1450
01:53:29,309 --> 01:53:33,146
Mr. Kaplan's untimely shooting has
acquired the authority of the printed word.

1451
01:53:33,646 --> 01:53:36,066
Everyone's been cooperating beautifully.

1452
01:53:36,149 --> 01:53:37,525
Now you can include me.

1453
01:53:38,234 --> 01:53:40,403
-I'm a cooperator.
-I'm most grateful.

1454
01:53:41,196 --> 01:53:44,199
-Care to do me a favor in return?
-Anything.

1455
01:53:44,282 --> 01:53:46,993
I'd like a drink.
Will you get me some bourbon?

1456
01:53:47,369 --> 01:53:48,787
A pint will do.

1457
01:53:49,621 --> 01:53:50,789
Can I join you?

1458
01:53:50,830 --> 01:53:54,125
If you're gonna join me,
you better make it a quart.

1459
01:53:54,167 --> 01:53:55,919
See you in a few minutes.

1460
01:55:01,276 --> 01:55:02,944
-Stop!
-Excuse me.

1461
01:55:04,237 --> 01:55:05,238
Stop.

1462
01:55:29,429 --> 01:55:33,516
-Don't you want me to take you up there?
-No, thanks. Never mind. This is fine.

1463
01:58:18,765 --> 01:58:21,810
-There's nothing to worry about.
-I just lost my head.

1464
01:58:22,394 --> 01:58:25,188
I'm not just saying this
to make you feel better.

1465
01:58:25,355 --> 01:58:26,564
I mean it.

1466
01:58:27,023 --> 01:58:29,109
I just didn't know what I was doing.

1467
01:58:29,192 --> 01:58:31,820
He wants to destroy you.
You had to protect yourself.

1468
01:58:31,903 --> 01:58:33,405
But not endanger you!

1469
01:58:33,488 --> 01:58:34,447
Rubbish.

1470
01:58:35,031 --> 01:58:39,035
Soon we'll be off together and I shall
dedicate myself to your happiness.

1471
01:58:40,662 --> 01:58:42,580
What's the situation, Leonard?

1472
01:58:42,831 --> 01:58:45,083
-About the plane, you mean?
-Of course.

1473
01:58:45,166 --> 01:58:46,751
What was the last report?

1474
01:58:46,918 --> 01:58:49,963
Over Whitestone on the hour.
6,000. Descending.

1475
01:58:50,213 --> 01:58:52,298
-About ten minutes?
-At the most.

1476
01:58:53,383 --> 01:58:54,467
And now....

1477
01:58:55,218 --> 01:58:58,221
I wonder if I might have a few words
of parting with you, sir?

1478
01:58:58,304 --> 01:58:59,514
Certainly.

1479
01:59:00,348 --> 01:59:01,641
In private?

1480
01:59:03,143 --> 01:59:04,352
I'll get my things.

1481
01:59:07,022 --> 01:59:08,773
How does one say goodbye...

1482
01:59:09,107 --> 01:59:10,650
...to one's right arm?

1483
01:59:17,365 --> 01:59:19,909
In your case,
you'll wish you had cut it off sooner.

1484
01:59:24,664 --> 01:59:26,916
I know you're terribly fond
of Miss Kendall....

1485
02:00:12,337 --> 02:00:14,339
It couldn't have been anything.

1486
02:00:17,717 --> 02:00:20,387
You must've had some doubts
about her yourself.

1487
02:00:21,930 --> 02:00:22,931
And still do.

1488
02:00:23,264 --> 02:00:24,391
Rubbish.

1489
02:00:25,016 --> 02:00:27,560
Why else would you've decided
not to tell her that...

1490
02:00:27,852 --> 02:00:30,563
...our little treasure here
has a bellyful of microfilm?

1491
02:00:30,647 --> 02:00:33,274
You seem to be trying to fill mine
with rotten apples.

1492
02:00:33,358 --> 02:00:36,069
Sometimes the truth does taste
like worms.

1493
02:00:36,111 --> 02:00:38,029
I've heard nothing but innuendoes.

1494
02:00:38,113 --> 02:00:41,825
Call it my "woman's intuition" if you will,
but I've never trusted neatness.

1495
02:00:41,908 --> 02:00:44,452
Neatness is always the result
of deliberate planning.

1496
02:00:44,536 --> 02:00:46,996
She shot him in a moment
of fear and anger.

1497
02:00:47,163 --> 02:00:49,040
You were there, you saw it yourself.

1498
02:00:49,290 --> 02:00:50,208
Yes.

1499
02:00:52,544 --> 02:00:56,631
And thereby wrapped everything up
into one very neat and tidy bundle.

1500
02:00:58,883 --> 02:01:01,720
A: She removed any doubts
you may have had about her.

1501
02:01:01,803 --> 02:01:04,431
What did you call it? Her "devotion"?

1502
02:01:04,681 --> 02:01:08,810
B: She gave herself an urgent reason
to be taken to the other side with you...

1503
02:01:08,893 --> 02:01:11,062
...in case you decided to change your mind.

1504
02:01:13,606 --> 02:01:15,233
I think you're jealous.

1505
02:01:15,483 --> 02:01:17,610
No, I mean it. I'm very touched.

1506
02:01:21,448 --> 02:01:22,574
Leonard!

1507
02:01:30,040 --> 02:01:31,624
The gun she shot Kaplan with.

1508
02:01:32,334 --> 02:01:33,752
I found it in her luggage.

1509
02:01:35,128 --> 02:01:39,090
It's an old trick. Shoot one of your own
to show that you're not one of them.

1510
02:01:39,632 --> 02:01:42,427
They've just freshened it up a bit
with blank cartridges.

1511
02:01:56,649 --> 02:01:57,817
What was that noise?

1512
02:02:00,695 --> 02:02:03,698
Yes, we wondered what it was, too.
Didn't we, Leonard?

1513
02:02:05,033 --> 02:02:07,160
Hurry down, darling. It's time to leave.

1514
02:02:07,619 --> 02:02:08,620
In a moment.

1515
02:02:17,921 --> 02:02:20,382
You're not taking her on that plane
with you?

1516
02:02:20,423 --> 02:02:21,591
Of course I am.

1517
02:02:22,759 --> 02:02:25,804
Like our friends,
l, too, believe in neatness, Leonard.

1518
02:02:26,596 --> 02:02:29,849
This matter is best disposed of
from a great height...

1519
02:02:30,809 --> 02:02:32,018
...over water.

1520
02:03:19,065 --> 02:03:21,234
How about a little champagne
before we go?

1521
02:03:21,317 --> 02:03:22,652
I'd love it.

1522
02:03:22,777 --> 02:03:24,362
It may not be cold enough.

1523
02:03:24,446 --> 02:03:26,197
On the rocks it'll be all right.

1524
02:03:26,281 --> 02:03:27,407
Are you sure?

1525
02:03:38,460 --> 02:03:39,586
R.O.T.

1526
02:03:55,602 --> 02:03:57,687
They're on to you.

1527
02:04:02,817 --> 02:04:05,445
I'm in your room.

1528
02:04:44,401 --> 02:04:45,485
To you, my dear...

1529
02:04:45,568 --> 02:04:47,987
...and all the lovely moments
we've had together.

1530
02:04:49,114 --> 02:04:49,948
Thank you.

1531
02:04:51,032 --> 02:04:51,908
There he is.

1532
02:04:59,833 --> 02:05:02,210
Jump in, Leonard. The champagne's fine.

1533
02:05:02,585 --> 02:05:04,129
There isn't time.

1534
02:05:04,212 --> 02:05:06,715
You always were a spoilsport, weren't you?

1535
02:05:07,132 --> 02:05:09,050
One of my most valuable attributes...

1536
02:05:09,217 --> 02:05:10,552
...as it now turns out.

1537
02:05:13,054 --> 02:05:17,017
It would please me if you'd think of me
as being along on this journey...

1538
02:05:17,726 --> 02:05:19,269
...if only in spirit.

1539
02:05:19,436 --> 02:05:20,437
I shall.

1540
02:05:28,445 --> 02:05:31,489
He's heading pretty far out
on the north flag and awfully high.

1541
02:05:32,574 --> 02:05:35,869
I guess he's going to play it safe
with a long, slow descent.

1542
02:05:36,911 --> 02:05:39,247
You couldn't ask
for a better night than this.

1543
02:05:39,330 --> 02:05:41,583
Ceiling and possibilities unlimited.

1544
02:05:43,710 --> 02:05:45,920
There he goes, starting his turn.

1545
02:05:47,255 --> 02:05:48,882
We'd better get moving.

1546
02:05:50,800 --> 02:05:54,179
He should have his wheels on the ground
inside of three minutes.

1547
02:05:58,183 --> 02:06:00,018
-Come along, Eve.
-All right.

1548
02:06:02,479 --> 02:06:05,857
I think I left my earrings upstairs.
I'll be right down.

1549
02:06:18,995 --> 02:06:21,664
We can go through the window.
There's a car downstairs!

1550
02:06:21,748 --> 02:06:24,000
What are you doing?
You'll ruin everything!

1551
02:06:24,084 --> 02:06:26,628
They know about the fake shooting!
They'll kill you!

1552
02:06:26,711 --> 02:06:27,796
What?

1553
02:06:27,921 --> 02:06:30,090
Leonard found the gun in your luggage!

1554
02:06:30,215 --> 02:06:33,426
The figure they got at the auction
is filled with microfilm!

1555
02:06:33,510 --> 02:06:35,929
That's how he's been getting it.

1556
02:06:36,763 --> 02:06:38,890
Whatever you do, don't get on that plane!

1557
02:06:46,439 --> 02:06:49,484
Don't worry, Anna.
Arrangements have been made.

1558
02:06:49,734 --> 02:06:52,445
You and your husband will be
in Canada by morning.

1559
02:06:52,487 --> 02:06:54,447
-Thank you very much, sir.
-Be careful.

1560
02:06:54,489 --> 02:06:55,281
We will.

1561
02:06:55,699 --> 02:06:56,700
God bless you!

1562
02:08:02,223 --> 02:08:03,391
Stay where you are!

1563
02:08:15,028 --> 02:08:15,987
Sit down.

1564
02:08:20,867 --> 02:08:24,204
As soon as the plane leaves,
my husband and Mr. Leonard will be back.

1565
02:08:52,315 --> 02:08:53,608
What is it?

1566
02:08:54,943 --> 02:08:56,736
I was wondering about my earrings.

1567
02:08:56,820 --> 02:08:58,196
They'll turn up.

1568
02:09:02,701 --> 02:09:05,704
When you return to New York,
say goodbye to my sister.

1569
02:09:06,037 --> 02:09:08,373
Thank her for her performance
as Mrs. Townsend.

1570
02:09:09,416 --> 02:09:12,836
Tell your knife-throwing chum
that I've reassured his wife.

1571
02:09:13,420 --> 02:09:14,671
That's all, Leonard.

1572
02:09:30,562 --> 02:09:31,771
Get that figure back!

1573
02:09:39,029 --> 02:09:41,448
The housekeeper had me pinned down
for five minutes...

1574
02:09:41,531 --> 02:09:43,908
...before I realized
it was that silly gun of yours.

1575
02:09:43,992 --> 02:09:45,785
-I see you've got the pumpkin.
-Yes.

1576
02:10:25,283 --> 02:10:27,994
This is no good.
We're on top of the monument.

1577
02:10:46,179 --> 02:10:47,931
-What'll we do?
-Climb down.

1578
02:10:48,014 --> 02:10:48,973
We can't.

1579
02:10:49,099 --> 02:10:51,267
Here they come. We have no choice.

1580
02:11:41,067 --> 02:11:42,986
If we ever get out of this alive...

1581
02:11:43,069 --> 02:11:45,530
...let's go back to New York
on the train together.

1582
02:11:45,613 --> 02:11:46,740
All right?

1583
02:11:47,240 --> 02:11:48,825
Is that a proposition?

1584
02:11:50,118 --> 02:11:51,911
It's a proposal, sweetie.

1585
02:11:53,121 --> 02:11:55,582
What happened to the first two marriages?

1586
02:11:56,499 --> 02:11:58,293
My wives divorced me.

1587
02:11:58,835 --> 02:11:59,669
Why?

1588
02:11:59,753 --> 02:12:02,297
I think they said I led too dull a life.

1589
02:12:03,423 --> 02:12:04,257
Come on.

1590
02:12:14,642 --> 02:12:15,727
Are you all right?

1591
02:12:26,321 --> 02:12:27,280
Come on.

1592
02:15:18,159 --> 02:15:18,910
Help!

1593
02:15:19,411 --> 02:15:20,537
Help me!

1594
02:15:52,360 --> 02:15:53,528
Thank you, Sergeant.

1595
02:15:53,611 --> 02:15:56,364
That wasn't very sporting,
using real bullets?

1596
02:15:56,656 --> 02:15:58,116
Here, reach. Now.

1597
02:15:59,367 --> 02:16:00,493
I'm trying.

1598
02:16:00,702 --> 02:16:01,995
Come on, I've got you.

1599
02:16:02,120 --> 02:16:04,164
-Up!
-I can't make it!

1600
02:16:04,539 --> 02:16:05,498
Yes, you can.

1601
02:16:05,540 --> 02:16:06,332
Come on.

1602
02:16:06,374 --> 02:16:07,292
Pull harder!

1603
02:16:07,709 --> 02:16:09,336
Come along, Mrs. Thornhill.

1604
02:16:13,715 --> 02:16:15,342
Roger, this is silly.

1605
02:16:15,675 --> 02:16:17,719
I know, but I'm sentimental.

1606
02:16:24,976 --> 02:16:28,980
THE END
NORTH BY NORTHWEST
